1
00:00:55,761 --> 00:00:57,554
[CHILDREN LAUGHING]

2
00:01:12,152 --> 00:01:13,278
[PHILLIPA SCREAMS]

3
00:01:25,248 --> 00:01:26,583
[SPEAKING IN JAPANESE]

4
00:01:33,548 --> 00:01:36,551
ATTENDANT [IN JAPANESE]:

5
00:02:09,876 --> 00:02:12,629
Are you here to kill me?

6
00:02:20,303 --> 00:02:22,806
I know what this is.

7
00:02:23,849 --> 00:02:29,020
I've seen one before.
Many, many years ago.

8
00:02:30,647 --> 00:02:34,526
It belonged to a man I met
in a half-remembered dream.

9
00:02:37,529 --> 00:02:42,659
A man possessed
of some radical notions.

10
00:02:46,496 --> 00:02:49,374
COBB:
What is the most resilient parasite?

11
00:02:49,666 --> 00:02:53,003
A bacteria? A virus?

12
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:54,838
An intestinal worm?

13
00:02:55,172 --> 00:02:56,423
ARTHUR:
Uh...

14
00:02:56,590 --> 00:02:58,216
What Mr. Cobb is trying to say--

15
00:02:58,383 --> 00:02:59,593
An idea.

16
00:03:00,886 --> 00:03:03,388
Resilient. Highly contagious.

17
00:03:03,555 --> 00:03:06,558
Once an idea has taken hold of the brain,
it's almost impossible to eradicate.

18
00:03:08,643 --> 00:03:12,272
An idea that is fully formed,
fully understood, that sticks.

19
00:03:12,439 --> 00:03:13,690
Right in there somewhere.

20
00:03:13,857 --> 00:03:15,400
For someone like you to steal?

21
00:03:15,567 --> 00:03:18,528
Yes. In the dream state,
your conscious defenses are lowered...

22
00:03:18,695 --> 00:03:21,114
...and that makes your thoughts
vulnerable to theft.

23
00:03:21,281 --> 00:03:22,532
It's called extraction.

24
00:03:22,699 --> 00:03:26,244
Mr. Saito, we can train
your subconscious to defend itself...

25
00:03:26,411 --> 00:03:28,914
...from even the most skilled extractor.

26
00:03:29,080 --> 00:03:30,248
How can you do that?

27
00:03:30,415 --> 00:03:33,251
Because I am the most skilled extractor.

28
00:03:33,710 --> 00:03:37,130
I know how to search your mind
and find your secrets. I know the tricks.

29
00:03:37,297 --> 00:03:40,217
And I can teach them to you,
so that even when you're asleep...

30
00:03:40,383 --> 00:03:42,719
...your defense is never down.

31
00:03:43,637 --> 00:03:47,557
Look, if you want my help, you're gonna
have to be completely open with me.

32
00:03:47,724 --> 00:03:51,061
I need to know my way around
your thoughts better than your wife...

33
00:03:51,228 --> 00:03:53,563
...better than your therapist,
better than anyone.

34
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:56,650
If this is a dream,
and you have a safe full of secrets...

35
00:03:56,816 --> 00:03:59,152
...I need to know what's in that safe.

36
00:03:59,319 --> 00:04:02,781
In order for this all to work,
you need to completely let me in.

37
00:04:05,659 --> 00:04:10,288
Enjoy your evening, gentlemen,
as I consider your proposal.

38
00:04:12,749 --> 00:04:13,792
He knows.

39
00:04:13,959 --> 00:04:15,585
[RUMBLING]

40
00:04:16,836 --> 00:04:18,296
What's going on up there?

41
00:04:26,221 --> 00:04:27,514
[MEN SHOUTING]

42
00:04:27,889 --> 00:04:29,474
[MACHINE HISSING]

43
00:04:39,859 --> 00:04:41,361
[EXPLOSION]

44
00:05:05,719 --> 00:05:06,970
[RUMBLING]

45
00:05:12,559 --> 00:05:15,145
Saito knows. He's playing with us.

46
00:05:15,562 --> 00:05:17,897
Doesn't matter. I can get it here.
Trust me.

47
00:05:18,064 --> 00:05:19,566
The information is in the safe.

48
00:05:19,733 --> 00:05:21,901
He looked right at it
when I mentioned secrets.

49
00:05:23,278 --> 00:05:24,321
What's she doing here?

50
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:30,118
Just head back to the room.
All right? I'll take care of this.

51
00:05:30,285 --> 00:05:32,329
Okay, make sure you do.
We're here to work.

52
00:05:40,754 --> 00:05:45,425
If I jumped, would I survive?

53
00:05:47,802 --> 00:05:50,180
With a clean dive, perhaps.

54
00:05:50,347 --> 00:05:52,015
Mal, what are you doing here?

55
00:05:52,182 --> 00:05:54,392
I thought you might be missing me.

56
00:05:54,684 --> 00:05:55,852
COBB:
You know that I am.

57
00:05:57,395 --> 00:05:59,856
But I can't trust you anymore.

58
00:06:00,607 --> 00:06:01,983
So what?

59
00:06:03,526 --> 00:06:05,737
MAL:
Looks like Arthur's taste.

60
00:06:05,904 --> 00:06:09,532
Actually, the subject is partial
to postwar British painters.

61
00:06:11,242 --> 00:06:12,952
Please, have a seat.

62
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:22,128
MAL:
Tell me...

63
00:06:22,545 --> 00:06:24,089
...do the children miss me?

64
00:06:28,426 --> 00:06:30,136
You can't imagine.

65
00:06:34,307 --> 00:06:35,392
What are you doing?

66
00:06:35,558 --> 00:06:37,102
Just getting some fresh air.

67
00:06:37,519 --> 00:06:39,270
Stay where you are, Mal.

68
00:06:48,405 --> 00:06:49,823
[GRUNTS]

69
00:06:52,992 --> 00:06:54,828
Goddamn it.

70
00:07:04,087 --> 00:07:05,296
[SOFT SCREECHING]

71
00:07:13,596 --> 00:07:14,597
[SILENCED GUNSHOT]

72
00:07:14,764 --> 00:07:16,015
[GUARD GRUNTS]

73
00:07:24,774 --> 00:07:26,401
[SILENCED GUNSHOT
THEN GUARD GRUNTS]

74
00:07:56,681 --> 00:07:58,266
SAITO:
Turn around.

75
00:07:58,433 --> 00:07:59,476
The gun, Dom.

76
00:08:08,735 --> 00:08:09,861
Please.

77
00:08:21,998 --> 00:08:24,334
Now the envelope, Mr. Cobb.

78
00:08:24,751 --> 00:08:28,171
Did she tell you?
Or have you known all along?

79
00:08:28,588 --> 00:08:33,551
That you're here to steal from me,
or that we are actually asleep?

80
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:40,058
I want to know the name
of your employer.

81
00:08:42,060 --> 00:08:45,939
Ah, there's no use threatening him
in a dream, right, Mal?

82
00:08:46,105 --> 00:08:48,566
That depends
on what you're threatening.

83
00:08:49,484 --> 00:08:51,653
Killing him would just wake him up.

84
00:08:52,403 --> 00:08:53,571
But pain...

85
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:56,699
[SCREAMS]

86
00:08:57,534 --> 00:08:59,410
Pain is in the mind.

87
00:09:00,119 --> 00:09:04,332
And judging by the decor,
we're in your mind, aren't we, Arthur?

88
00:09:07,794 --> 00:09:08,878
[GRUNTS]

89
00:09:15,718 --> 00:09:18,847
- What are you doing? It's too soon.
- But the dream's collapsing.

90
00:09:19,013 --> 00:09:22,433
I'm gonna try to keep Saito under
a little bit longer. We're almost there.

91
00:09:22,851 --> 00:09:23,935
[SILENCED GUNFIRE]

92
00:09:36,656 --> 00:09:40,618
He was close. Very close.

93
00:09:43,246 --> 00:09:44,455
[SCREAMS]

94
00:09:53,756 --> 00:09:54,757
[YELLS]

95
00:09:54,924 --> 00:09:56,509
Stop him!

96
00:10:01,139 --> 00:10:02,891
[GUNFIRE]

97
00:10:10,607 --> 00:10:11,858
[GRUNTS]

98
00:10:18,114 --> 00:10:19,574
[HISSING]

99
00:10:20,867 --> 00:10:22,160
[SAITO GROANS]

100
00:10:22,327 --> 00:10:24,495
This isn't gonna work. Wake him up.

101
00:10:37,133 --> 00:10:38,468
[GRUNTS]

102
00:10:45,308 --> 00:10:47,268
He won't wake.

103
00:10:51,731 --> 00:10:53,024
- Give him the kick.
NASH: What?

104
00:10:53,566 --> 00:10:54,609
Dunk him.

105
00:10:54,776 --> 00:10:55,985
[GUN COCKS]

106
00:11:01,824 --> 00:11:03,368
[WOOD GROANING]

107
00:11:30,978 --> 00:11:32,563
[SAITO GRUNTING]

108
00:11:39,153 --> 00:11:40,530
COBB:
He's out.

109
00:11:42,532 --> 00:11:44,409
[SHOUTING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

110
00:11:45,827 --> 00:11:47,870
You came prepared, hmm?

111
00:11:48,037 --> 00:11:51,874
Not even my head of security knows
this apartment. How did you find it?

112
00:11:52,542 --> 00:11:56,254
It's difficult for a man of your position
to keep a love nest like this secret...

113
00:11:56,421 --> 00:11:58,965
...particularly where there's
a married woman involved.

114
00:11:59,132 --> 00:12:01,092
- She would never...
- Yet here we are.

115
00:12:02,176 --> 00:12:04,262
- With a dilemma.
- They're getting closer.

116
00:12:04,429 --> 00:12:07,223
- You got what you came for.
COBB: Well, that's not true.

117
00:12:07,390 --> 00:12:10,059
You left out a key piece of information,
didn't you?

118
00:12:10,226 --> 00:12:13,396
You held something back
because you knew what we were up to.

119
00:12:13,563 --> 00:12:16,441
- Question is, why'd you let us in at all?
- An audition.

120
00:12:17,650 --> 00:12:20,570
- An audition for what?
- Doesn't matter. You failed.

121
00:12:20,737 --> 00:12:23,448
We extracted every bit of information
you had in there.

122
00:12:23,865 --> 00:12:26,159
But your deception was obvious.

123
00:12:32,415 --> 00:12:33,666
[MACHINE HISSING]

124
00:12:56,773 --> 00:13:00,651
[EDITH PIAF'S
"NON, JE NE REGRETTE RIEN" PLAYING]

125
00:13:03,279 --> 00:13:05,281
[SONG ECHOING]

126
00:13:07,033 --> 00:13:11,454
- So leave me and go.
- You don't seem to understand, Mr. Saito.

127
00:13:11,621 --> 00:13:15,875
That corporation that hired us,
they won't accept failure.

128
00:13:16,042 --> 00:13:18,169
We won't last two days.

129
00:13:20,129 --> 00:13:21,297
Cobb?

130
00:13:21,464 --> 00:13:24,634
Looks like I'm gonna have to do this
a little more simply.

131
00:13:24,801 --> 00:13:28,554
Tell us what you know!
Tell us what you know, now!

132
00:13:29,472 --> 00:13:31,182
[LAUGHING]

133
00:13:31,933 --> 00:13:34,811
SAITO:
I've always hated this carpet.

134
00:13:35,436 --> 00:13:39,315
It's stained and frayed
in such distinctive ways.

135
00:13:40,817 --> 00:13:44,153
But very definitely made of wool.

136
00:13:44,987 --> 00:13:46,697
Right now...

137
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:50,159
...I'm lying on polyester.

138
00:13:54,038 --> 00:13:58,876
Which means I'm not lying
on my carpet, in my apartment.

139
00:13:59,752 --> 00:14:03,005
You have lived up to your reputation,
Mr. Cobb.

140
00:14:03,464 --> 00:14:05,508
I'm still dreaming.

141
00:14:07,176 --> 00:14:08,886
[ALARM BEEPING]

142
00:14:12,181 --> 00:14:14,016
- How'd it go?
- Not good.

143
00:14:19,397 --> 00:14:23,526
Dream within a dream, huh?
I'm impressed.

144
00:14:24,777 --> 00:14:28,281
But in my dream, you play by my rules.

145
00:14:28,447 --> 00:14:30,700
Ah, yes, but you see, Mr. Saito...

146
00:14:30,867 --> 00:14:32,285
We're not in your dream.

147
00:14:32,451 --> 00:14:34,036
We're in mine.

148
00:14:34,203 --> 00:14:35,746
[MEN SHOUTING]

149
00:14:36,873 --> 00:14:38,624
[ALARM BEEPING FASTER]

150
00:14:41,294 --> 00:14:42,628
[NASH YELLS]

151
00:14:43,963 --> 00:14:46,048
Asshole.
How do you mess up the carpet?

152
00:14:46,215 --> 00:14:48,551
- It wasn't my fault.
- You're the architect.

153
00:14:48,718 --> 00:14:50,845
I didn't know
he was gonna rub his cheek on it!

154
00:14:51,012 --> 00:14:52,096
That's enough.

155
00:14:53,222 --> 00:14:54,724
You. What the hell was all that?

156
00:14:55,224 --> 00:14:58,102
- I have it under control.
- I'd hate to see you out of control.

157
00:14:58,269 --> 00:15:02,064
We don't have time for this.
I'm getting off at Kyoto.

158
00:15:02,231 --> 00:15:04,066
ARTHUR:
He's not gonna check every compartment.

159
00:15:04,233 --> 00:15:05,860
Yeah, well, I don't like trains.

160
00:15:06,027 --> 00:15:08,779
Listen. Every man for himself.

161
00:15:08,779 --> 00:15:18,779
<b>Subtitle | www.movieddl.me</b>

162
00:16:17,473 --> 00:16:19,141
[PHONE RINGS]

163
00:16:23,145 --> 00:16:24,146
Yes, hello?

164
00:16:24,355 --> 00:16:26,983
PHILLIPA: <i>Hi, Daddy.</i>
JAMES: <i>Hi, Dad.</i>

165
00:16:27,483 --> 00:16:31,112
Hey, guys. Hey. How are you?
How you doing, huh?

166
00:16:31,278 --> 00:16:33,489
PHILLIPA: <i>Good.</i>
JAMES: <i>Okay, I guess.</i>

167
00:16:33,656 --> 00:16:37,410
Okay? Who's just okay?
Is that you, James?

168
00:16:37,576 --> 00:16:41,497
JAMES:
<i>Yeah. When are you coming home, Dad?</i>

169
00:16:42,498 --> 00:16:47,128
Well, I can't, sweetheart. I can't.
Not for a while, remember?

170
00:16:47,294 --> 00:16:48,629
JAMES:
<i>Why?</i>

171
00:16:48,963 --> 00:16:54,510
Look, I told you, I'm away
because I'm working, right?

172
00:16:54,844 --> 00:16:57,680
PHILLIPA:
<i>Grandma says you're never coming back.</i>

173
00:16:58,347 --> 00:17:00,224
COBB:
<i>Phillipa, is that you?</i>

174
00:17:01,183 --> 00:17:03,519
Put Grandma on the phone for me,
will you?

175
00:17:03,686 --> 00:17:05,396
PHILLIPA:
<i>She's shaking her head.</i>

176
00:17:07,523 --> 00:17:10,026
Well, just hope she's wrong about that.

177
00:17:10,901 --> 00:17:12,111
JAMES:
<i>Daddy?</i>

178
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:13,863
Yeah, James?

179
00:17:14,030 --> 00:17:15,698
JAMES:
<i>ls Mommy with you?</i>

180
00:17:18,325 --> 00:17:20,536
James, we talked about this.

181
00:17:21,829 --> 00:17:24,165
Mommy's not here anymore.

182
00:17:25,207 --> 00:17:26,459
JAMES:
<i>Where?</i>

183
00:17:28,210 --> 00:17:30,838
WOMAN:
<i>That's enough, kids. Say bye-bye.</i>

184
00:17:31,005 --> 00:17:34,050
Listen, I'm gonna send some presents
with Grandpa, all right?

185
00:17:34,216 --> 00:17:35,217
And you be good, you be--

186
00:17:35,384 --> 00:17:37,053
[DIAL TONE]

187
00:17:42,892 --> 00:17:43,976
[KNOCKING ON DOOR]

188
00:17:45,728 --> 00:17:47,938
- Our ride's on the roof.
- Right.

189
00:17:55,112 --> 00:17:56,280
Hey, are you okay?

190
00:17:57,031 --> 00:17:58,324
Yeah. Yeah, I'm fine. Why?

191
00:17:58,491 --> 00:18:00,910
Well, down in the dream,
Mal showing up.

192
00:18:01,077 --> 00:18:04,330
Look, I'm, uh, sorry about your leg.
Won't happen again.

193
00:18:04,497 --> 00:18:08,084
- It's getting worse, isn't it?
- One apology's all you're getting, all right?

194
00:18:08,250 --> 00:18:10,753
- Where's Nash?
- He hasn't shown. You wanna wait?

195
00:18:10,920 --> 00:18:13,380
We were supposed to deliver
Saito's expansion plans...

196
00:18:13,547 --> 00:18:15,299
...to Cobol Engineering two hours ago.

197
00:18:15,466 --> 00:18:18,719
By now, they know we failed.
It's time we disappear.

198
00:18:19,261 --> 00:18:21,764
- Where you gonna go?
- Buenos Aires.

199
00:18:21,931 --> 00:18:26,185
I can lie low there, maybe sniff out
a job when things quiet down. You?

200
00:18:26,352 --> 00:18:27,561
Stateside.

201
00:18:27,770 --> 00:18:29,271
Send my regards.

202
00:18:35,611 --> 00:18:39,156
He sold you out. Thought to come
to me and bargain for his life.

203
00:18:41,325 --> 00:18:43,119
So I offer you the satisfaction.

204
00:18:47,081 --> 00:18:49,625
It's not the way I deal with things.

205
00:18:52,670 --> 00:18:54,171
[HELICOPTER STARTING]

206
00:19:01,887 --> 00:19:04,765
- What will you do with him?
- Nothing.

207
00:19:05,141 --> 00:19:07,643
But I can't speak for Cobol Engineering.

208
00:19:22,158 --> 00:19:25,161
- What do you want from us?
- Inception.

209
00:19:26,787 --> 00:19:29,165
- Is it possible?
- Of course not.

210
00:19:29,331 --> 00:19:31,542
If you can steal an idea
from someone's mind...

211
00:19:31,709 --> 00:19:33,586
...why can't you plant one there instead?

212
00:19:33,752 --> 00:19:35,838
Okay, here's me planting
an idea in your head.

213
00:19:36,005 --> 00:19:39,341
I say, "Don't think about elephants."
What are you thinking about?

214
00:19:39,508 --> 00:19:40,509
Elephants.

215
00:19:40,676 --> 00:19:43,846
Right. But it's not your idea,
because you know I gave it to you.

216
00:19:44,054 --> 00:19:47,016
The subject's mind can always trace
the genesis of the idea.

217
00:19:47,183 --> 00:19:50,394
- True inspiration's impossible to fake.
- That's not true.

218
00:19:52,938 --> 00:19:56,025
- Can you do it?
- Are you offering me a choice?

219
00:19:56,192 --> 00:19:58,861
Because I can find my own way
to square things with Cobol.

220
00:19:59,028 --> 00:20:01,071
Then you do have a choice.

221
00:20:01,238 --> 00:20:03,032
Then I choose to leave, sir.

222
00:20:07,912 --> 00:20:09,622
Tell the crew where you want to go.

223
00:20:14,877 --> 00:20:15,961
Hey, Mr. Cobb.

224
00:20:19,131 --> 00:20:21,217
How would you like to go home?

225
00:20:22,009 --> 00:20:25,304
To America. To your children.

226
00:20:25,721 --> 00:20:29,058
You can't fix that. No one can.

227
00:20:29,225 --> 00:20:32,478
- Just like inception.
- Cobb, come on.

228
00:20:36,232 --> 00:20:39,109
- How complex is the idea?
- Simple enough.

229
00:20:39,276 --> 00:20:42,821
No idea is simple when you need
to plant it in somebody else's mind.

230
00:20:43,239 --> 00:20:46,742
My main competitor is
an old man in poor health.

231
00:20:46,909 --> 00:20:50,663
His son will soon inherit control
of the corporation.

232
00:20:51,580 --> 00:20:55,125
I need him to decide to break up
his father's empire.

233
00:20:55,834 --> 00:20:58,504
- Cobb, we should walk away from this.
- Hold on.

234
00:21:00,589 --> 00:21:03,842
If I were to do this,
if I even could do it...

235
00:21:04,009 --> 00:21:05,928
...I'd need a guarantee.

236
00:21:06,095 --> 00:21:09,348
- How do I know you can deliver?
- You don't.

237
00:21:09,932 --> 00:21:11,433
But I can.

238
00:21:12,017 --> 00:21:16,605
So do you want to take a leap of faith...

239
00:21:17,022 --> 00:21:20,859
...or become an old man,
filled with regret...

240
00:21:21,026 --> 00:21:23,612
...waiting to die alone?

241
00:21:26,699 --> 00:21:30,995
Assemble your team, Mr. Cobb.
And choose your people more wisely.

242
00:21:39,128 --> 00:21:40,963
Look, I know how much
you wanna go home.

243
00:21:43,757 --> 00:21:45,467
This can't be done.

244
00:21:45,634 --> 00:21:48,929
Yes, it can.
Just have to go deep enough.

245
00:21:49,138 --> 00:21:50,639
You don't know that.

246
00:21:51,974 --> 00:21:53,809
I've done it before.

247
00:21:55,019 --> 00:21:56,186
Who'd you do it to?

248
00:22:01,442 --> 00:22:03,444
Why are we going to Paris?

249
00:22:05,029 --> 00:22:06,739
We're gonna need a new architect.

250
00:22:07,323 --> 00:22:09,033
[BELLS TOLLING]

251
00:22:23,672 --> 00:22:25,716
COBB:
You never did like your office, did you?

252
00:22:27,509 --> 00:22:30,554
No space to think
in that broom cupboard.

253
00:22:32,431 --> 00:22:34,683
Is it safe for you to be here?

254
00:22:35,893 --> 00:22:38,312
Extradition between France
and the United States...

255
00:22:38,479 --> 00:22:40,689
...is a bureaucratic nightmare,
you know that.

256
00:22:40,856 --> 00:22:43,609
I think they might find a way
to make it work in your case.

257
00:22:43,776 --> 00:22:48,280
Look, I, uh, brought these for you to give
to the kids when you have a chance.

258
00:22:48,447 --> 00:22:51,033
It'll take more than
the occasional stuffed animal...

259
00:22:51,200 --> 00:22:54,203
...to convince those children
they still have a father.

260
00:22:54,370 --> 00:22:57,206
I'm just doing what I know.
I'm doing what you taught me.

261
00:22:57,373 --> 00:22:58,874
I never taught you to be a thief.

262
00:22:59,041 --> 00:23:01,251
No, you taught me to navigate
people's minds.

263
00:23:01,418 --> 00:23:02,711
But after what happened...

264
00:23:02,878 --> 00:23:06,465
...there weren't a whole lot of legitimate
ways for me to use that skill.

265
00:23:11,595 --> 00:23:13,097
What are you doing here, Dom?

266
00:23:15,224 --> 00:23:17,601
I think I found a way home.

267
00:23:17,768 --> 00:23:21,355
It's a job
for some very, very powerful people.

268
00:23:21,563 --> 00:23:25,984
People who I believe can fix
my charges permanently.

269
00:23:26,777 --> 00:23:28,070
But I need your help.

270
00:23:28,737 --> 00:23:32,074
You're here to corrupt
one of my brightest and best.

271
00:23:32,241 --> 00:23:35,119
You know what I'm offering.
Let them decide for themselves.

272
00:23:35,285 --> 00:23:36,745
- Money.
- Not just money.

273
00:23:36,912 --> 00:23:38,247
You remember.

274
00:23:38,414 --> 00:23:41,667
It's the chance to build cathedrals,
entire cities...

275
00:23:41,834 --> 00:23:43,585
...things that never existed...

276
00:23:43,752 --> 00:23:46,964
...things that couldn't exist
in the real world.

277
00:23:47,131 --> 00:23:52,302
So you want me to let someone else
follow you into your fantasy?

278
00:23:52,469 --> 00:23:54,430
They don't actually come into the dream.

279
00:23:54,596 --> 00:23:57,891
They just design the levels
and teach them to the dreamers. That's all.

280
00:23:58,058 --> 00:23:59,268
Design it yourself.

281
00:24:01,937 --> 00:24:03,439
Mal won't let me.

282
00:24:09,653 --> 00:24:11,780
Come back to reality, Dom.

283
00:24:13,115 --> 00:24:15,701
- Please.
- Reality.

284
00:24:16,076 --> 00:24:18,620
Those kids, your grandchildren...

285
00:24:18,787 --> 00:24:22,791
...they're waiting for their father to come
back home. That's their reality.

286
00:24:22,958 --> 00:24:26,128
And this job, this last job,
that's how I get there.

287
00:24:26,670 --> 00:24:30,299
I would not be standing here
if I knew any other way.

288
00:24:33,469 --> 00:24:36,388
I need an architect
who's as good as I was.

289
00:24:40,601 --> 00:24:42,603
I've got somebody better.

290
00:24:42,769 --> 00:24:43,812
<i>Ariadne?</i>

291
00:24:46,398 --> 00:24:48,942
I'd like you to meet Mr. Cobb.

292
00:24:49,318 --> 00:24:50,444
Pleased to meet you.

293
00:24:50,611 --> 00:24:55,282
If you have a few moments, Mr. Cobb has
a job offer he'd like to discuss with you.

294
00:24:55,449 --> 00:24:56,492
A work placement?

295
00:24:56,867 --> 00:24:58,160
Not exactly.

296
00:24:58,327 --> 00:24:59,661
I have a test for you.

297
00:25:00,412 --> 00:25:02,498
You're not gonna tell me anything first?

298
00:25:02,664 --> 00:25:05,292
Before I describe the job,
I have to know you can do it.

299
00:25:05,459 --> 00:25:08,670
- Why?
- It's not, strictly speaking, legal.

300
00:25:11,757 --> 00:25:16,428
You have two minutes to design a maze
that it takes one minute to solve.

301
00:25:18,347 --> 00:25:19,515
Stop.

302
00:25:21,934 --> 00:25:23,143
Again.

303
00:25:25,270 --> 00:25:26,605
Stop.

304
00:25:29,775 --> 00:25:31,860
You're gonna have to do better than that.

305
00:25:44,873 --> 00:25:46,124
That's more like it.

306
00:26:06,311 --> 00:26:09,231
COBB: <i>They say we only use a fraction
of our brain's true potential.</i>

307
00:26:09,398 --> 00:26:11,066
<i>Now, that's when we're awake.</i>

308
00:26:11,233 --> 00:26:14,236
When we're asleep,
our mind can do almost anything.

309
00:26:14,403 --> 00:26:15,404
Such as?

310
00:26:15,571 --> 00:26:19,575
Imagine you're designing a building.
You consciously create each aspect.

311
00:26:19,741 --> 00:26:23,579
But sometimes, it feels like it's almost
creating itself, if you know what I mean.

312
00:26:23,745 --> 00:26:25,747
Yeah, like I'm discovering it.

313
00:26:25,914 --> 00:26:28,166
Genuine inspiration, right?

314
00:26:28,333 --> 00:26:31,670
Now, in a dream,
our mind continuously does this.

315
00:26:31,837 --> 00:26:36,466
We create and perceive
our world simultaneously.

316
00:26:36,633 --> 00:26:40,053
And our mind does this so well
that we don't even know it's happening.

317
00:26:40,220 --> 00:26:42,931
That allows us to get right
in the middle of that process.

318
00:26:43,098 --> 00:26:45,475
- How?
- By taking over the creating part.

319
00:26:45,642 --> 00:26:47,269
Now, this is where I need you.

320
00:26:47,436 --> 00:26:49,813
You create the world of the dream.

321
00:26:49,980 --> 00:26:55,360
We bring the subject into that dream,
and they fill it with their subconscious.

322
00:26:55,527 --> 00:27:00,198
How could I ever acquire enough detail
to make them think that it's reality?

323
00:27:00,365 --> 00:27:03,827
Well, dreams, they feel real
while we're in them, right?

324
00:27:03,994 --> 00:27:07,706
It's only when we wake up that we realize
something was actually strange.

325
00:27:09,166 --> 00:27:10,584
Let me ask you a question.

326
00:27:10,751 --> 00:27:14,129
You never really remember
the beginning of a dream, do you?

327
00:27:14,296 --> 00:27:17,341
You always wind up
right in the middle of what's going on.

328
00:27:17,507 --> 00:27:18,550
I guess, yeah.

329
00:27:18,717 --> 00:27:20,302
So how did we end up here?

330
00:27:20,677 --> 00:27:22,763
Well, we just came from the, uh...

331
00:27:22,971 --> 00:27:26,391
Think about it, Ariadne.
How did you get here?

332
00:27:26,975 --> 00:27:28,894
Where are you right now?

333
00:27:32,189 --> 00:27:33,357
We're dreaming?

334
00:27:33,523 --> 00:27:35,984
You're in the middle
of the workshop, sleeping.

335
00:27:36,151 --> 00:27:39,112
This is your first lesson
in shared dreaming. Stay calm.

336
00:27:39,279 --> 00:27:40,489
[CUP RATTLING]

337
00:27:40,989 --> 00:27:42,699
[RUMBLING]

338
00:28:16,441 --> 00:28:18,568
If it's just a dream,
then why are you--?

339
00:28:18,735 --> 00:28:20,028
["NON, JE NE REGRETTE RIEN" PLAYING]

340
00:28:20,195 --> 00:28:21,947
COBB:
Because it's never just a dream, is it?

341
00:28:22,114 --> 00:28:25,409
And a face full of glass hurts like hell.
When you're in it, it feels real.

342
00:28:25,575 --> 00:28:27,786
ARTHUR: That's why the military
developed dream sharing.

343
00:28:27,953 --> 00:28:31,665
It was a training program for soldiers
to shoot, stab and strangle each other...

344
00:28:31,832 --> 00:28:32,958
...and then wake up.

345
00:28:33,250 --> 00:28:35,043
ARIADNE:
How did architects become involved?

346
00:28:35,210 --> 00:28:37,963
COBB: Well, someone had to design
the dreams, right?

347
00:28:39,089 --> 00:28:40,382
Why don't you give us
another five minutes?

348
00:28:42,426 --> 00:28:46,388
Five minutes? What--? We were talking
for, like, at least an hour.

349
00:28:46,555 --> 00:28:48,807
In a dream, your mind
functions more quickly...

350
00:28:48,974 --> 00:28:51,810
...therefore time seems
to feel more slow.

351
00:28:51,977 --> 00:28:55,355
Five minutes in the real world
gives you an hour in the dream.

352
00:28:56,064 --> 00:28:59,151
Why don't you see what you
can get up to in five minutes?

353
00:29:01,153 --> 00:29:02,612
[MACHINE HISSING]

354
00:29:05,073 --> 00:29:07,993
COBB: You've got the basic layout.
Bookstore, café.

355
00:29:08,160 --> 00:29:09,828
Almost everything else is here too.

356
00:29:09,995 --> 00:29:12,914
ARIADNE: Who are the people?
- Projections of my subconscious.

357
00:29:13,081 --> 00:29:14,249
- Yours?
- Yes.

358
00:29:14,416 --> 00:29:16,960
Remember, you are the dreamer.
You build this world.

359
00:29:17,127 --> 00:29:19,796
I am the subject. My mind populates it.

360
00:29:19,963 --> 00:29:21,882
You can literally talk
to my subconscious.

361
00:29:22,048 --> 00:29:24,926
That's one of the ways we extract
information from the subject.

362
00:29:25,093 --> 00:29:26,386
How else do you do it?

363
00:29:26,595 --> 00:29:30,557
By creating something secure,
like a bank vault or a jail.

364
00:29:30,724 --> 00:29:34,436
The mind automatically fills it
with information it's trying to protect.

365
00:29:34,603 --> 00:29:37,397
- You understand?
- Then you break in and steal it?

366
00:29:37,564 --> 00:29:38,607
Well...

367
00:29:38,774 --> 00:29:43,278
I guess I thought that the dream space
would be all about the visual...

368
00:29:43,445 --> 00:29:45,280
...but it's more about the feel of it.

369
00:29:45,781 --> 00:29:49,910
My question is what happens when you
start messing with the physics of it all?

370
00:29:51,578 --> 00:29:53,455
[RUMBLING THEN METAL GROANING]

371
00:30:31,243 --> 00:30:32,828
It's something, isn't it?

372
00:30:33,912 --> 00:30:35,205
Yes, it is.

373
00:30:59,104 --> 00:31:00,689
ARIADNE:
Why are they all looking at me?

374
00:31:00,856 --> 00:31:04,359
Because my subconscious feels that
someone else is creating this world.

375
00:31:04,526 --> 00:31:08,822
The more you change things, the quicker
the projections start to converge on you.

376
00:31:08,989 --> 00:31:10,031
Converge?

377
00:31:10,198 --> 00:31:12,284
They sense the foreign nature
of the dreamer.

378
00:31:12,450 --> 00:31:15,120
They attack, like white blood cells
fighting an infection.

379
00:31:15,287 --> 00:31:18,123
- What, they're gonna attack us?
- No, no.

380
00:31:18,707 --> 00:31:20,208
Just you.

381
00:31:21,501 --> 00:31:22,961
[METAL CREAKING]

382
00:31:24,170 --> 00:31:28,425
This is great, but I'm telling you,
if you keep changing things like this...

383
00:31:31,928 --> 00:31:34,890
Jeez, mind telling your subconscious
to take it easy?

384
00:31:35,056 --> 00:31:37,726
It's my subconscious.
Remember? I can't control it.

385
00:32:31,404 --> 00:32:32,781
Very impressive.

386
00:32:44,501 --> 00:32:48,046
I know this bridge.
This place is real, isn't it?

387
00:32:48,213 --> 00:32:50,548
Yeah, I cross it every day
to get to the college.

388
00:32:50,715 --> 00:32:54,135
Never re-create places from your memory.
Always imagine new places.

389
00:32:54,302 --> 00:32:56,137
You draw from stuff you know, right?

390
00:32:56,304 --> 00:33:00,517
Only use details. A streetlamp
or a phone booth. Never entire areas.

391
00:33:00,684 --> 00:33:02,811
- Why not?
- Building a dream from your memory...

392
00:33:02,978 --> 00:33:06,481
...is the easiest way to lose your grasp
on what's real and what is a dream.

393
00:33:06,648 --> 00:33:08,733
- Is that what happened to you?
- Listen to me.

394
00:33:08,900 --> 00:33:10,902
This has nothing to do
with me, understand?

395
00:33:11,069 --> 00:33:13,029
That why you need me
to build your dreams?

396
00:33:13,196 --> 00:33:15,740
Hey, get off of her. Back up. Back up.

397
00:33:15,907 --> 00:33:17,534
- Cobb! Cobb!
- Get off of her!

398
00:33:17,701 --> 00:33:19,661
- Let me go! Let me go!
- Mal!

399
00:33:20,161 --> 00:33:22,247
- Mal!
- Cobb! Wake me up!

400
00:33:22,414 --> 00:33:24,207
- Wake me up! Wake me up!
- No!

401
00:33:24,374 --> 00:33:25,875
- Mal, no! No!
- Wake me up!

402
00:33:26,042 --> 00:33:27,335
[GASPING]

403
00:33:27,502 --> 00:33:31,506
Hey, hey, hey. Look at me.
You're okay. You're okay.

404
00:33:31,673 --> 00:33:34,884
- Hey.
- Why wouldn't I wake up?

405
00:33:35,176 --> 00:33:36,803
There was still time on the clock.

406
00:33:36,970 --> 00:33:39,514
You can't wake up from
within the dream unless you die.

407
00:33:39,681 --> 00:33:41,266
- She'll need a totem.
ARIADNE: What?

408
00:33:41,433 --> 00:33:42,892
A totem, it's a small personal--

409
00:33:43,059 --> 00:33:45,603
That's some subconscious
you've got on you, Cobb!

410
00:33:45,770 --> 00:33:47,605
- She's a real charmer.
- Oh.

411
00:33:47,772 --> 00:33:50,400
- I see you met Mrs. Cobb.
- She's his wife?

412
00:33:50,567 --> 00:33:54,320
Yeah. So a totem. You need
a small object, potentially heavy.

413
00:33:54,487 --> 00:33:56,948
Something you can have on you
that no one else knows.

414
00:33:57,115 --> 00:33:58,366
ARIADNE: Like a coin?
ARTHUR: No.

415
00:33:58,533 --> 00:34:03,204
It needs to be more unique than that.
Like, this is a loaded die.

416
00:34:04,289 --> 00:34:07,208
No, I can't let you touch it.
That would defeat the purpose.

417
00:34:07,375 --> 00:34:11,546
See, only I know the balance and
the weight of this particular loaded die.

418
00:34:11,713 --> 00:34:13,548
That way,
when you look at your totem...

419
00:34:13,715 --> 00:34:16,843
...you know beyond a doubt that
you're not in someone else's dream.

420
00:34:20,263 --> 00:34:24,559
I don't know if you can't see what's
going on, or if you just don't want to...

421
00:34:24,726 --> 00:34:28,271
...but Cobb has some serious problems
that he's tried to bury down there.

422
00:34:28,438 --> 00:34:32,400
And I'm not about to just open my mind
to someone like that.

423
00:34:37,238 --> 00:34:38,364
[DOOR CLOSES]

424
00:34:38,531 --> 00:34:41,910
She'll be back. I've never seen anyone
pick it up that quickly before.

425
00:34:42,410 --> 00:34:46,581
Reality's not gonna be enough for her now,
and when she comes back...

426
00:34:47,248 --> 00:34:49,918
When she comes back,
you're gonna have her building mazes.

427
00:34:50,085 --> 00:34:52,378
- Where are you gonna be?
- I gotta go visit Eames.

428
00:34:52,545 --> 00:34:55,673
Eames? No, he's in Mombasa.
It's Cobol's back yard.

429
00:34:55,882 --> 00:34:57,425
It's a necessary risk.

430
00:34:57,842 --> 00:35:01,096
- Well, there's plenty of good thieves.
- We don't just need a thief.

431
00:35:02,222 --> 00:35:03,264
We need a forger.

432
00:35:11,940 --> 00:35:14,526
Rub them together all you want,
they're not gonna breed.

433
00:35:15,110 --> 00:35:16,111
You never know.

434
00:35:17,070 --> 00:35:19,114
COBB:
I'm gonna get you a drink.

435
00:35:21,282 --> 00:35:22,617
You're buying.

436
00:35:29,124 --> 00:35:31,417
- Your spelling hasn't improved.
- Piss off.

437
00:35:31,584 --> 00:35:33,545
How's your handwriting?

438
00:35:33,711 --> 00:35:35,547
- It's versatile.
- Good.

439
00:35:35,713 --> 00:35:36,965
Thank you very much.

440
00:35:37,632 --> 00:35:38,967
Inception.

441
00:35:39,217 --> 00:35:42,053
Now, before you bother telling me
it's impossible, let me--

442
00:35:42,220 --> 00:35:45,515
No, it's perfectly possible.
It's just bloody difficult.

443
00:35:45,682 --> 00:35:48,977
Interesting. Because Arthur keeps
telling me it can't be done.

444
00:35:49,394 --> 00:35:52,355
Hmm. Arthur. You still working
with that stick in the mud?

445
00:35:52,522 --> 00:35:54,232
He is good at what he does, right?

446
00:35:54,399 --> 00:35:56,484
Oh, he's the best,
but he has no imagination.

447
00:35:56,651 --> 00:35:57,652
Not like you.

448
00:35:57,819 --> 00:36:00,822
Listen, if you're gonna perform inception,
you need imagination.

449
00:36:00,989 --> 00:36:04,534
Let me ask you something.
Have you done it before?

450
00:36:04,701 --> 00:36:08,329
We tried it. Uh, we got the idea in place,
but it didn't take.

451
00:36:08,496 --> 00:36:11,833
- You didn't plant it deep enough?
- No, it's not just about depth.

452
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:14,002
You need the simplest version
of the idea...

453
00:36:14,169 --> 00:36:17,839
...in order for it to grow naturally
in your subject's mind. It's a subtle art.

454
00:36:18,006 --> 00:36:20,550
So, what is this idea
that you need to plant?

455
00:36:20,717 --> 00:36:24,929
We need the heir of a major corporation
to dissolve his father's empire.

456
00:36:25,096 --> 00:36:27,515
Right there you have
various political motivations...

457
00:36:27,682 --> 00:36:29,684
...and anti-monopolistic sentiments
and so forth.

458
00:36:29,851 --> 00:36:31,686
But all of that stuff, it's, um...

459
00:36:31,853 --> 00:36:34,731
It's really at the mercy of your
subject's prejudice, you see?

460
00:36:34,898 --> 00:36:37,400
What you have to do is
start at the absolute basic.

461
00:36:37,567 --> 00:36:39,152
Which is what?

462
00:36:39,652 --> 00:36:41,529
The relationship with the father.

463
00:36:43,740 --> 00:36:45,408
- Do you have a chemist?
- No, not yet.

464
00:36:45,575 --> 00:36:48,661
Right. Okay, well,
there's a man here, Yusuf.

465
00:36:48,828 --> 00:36:52,248
He, uh, formulates his own versions
of the compounds.

466
00:36:52,415 --> 00:36:53,708
Why don't you take me there?

467
00:36:53,875 --> 00:36:56,961
Once you've lost your tail.
The man at the bar.

468
00:36:57,128 --> 00:36:58,588
COBB:
Cobol Engineering.

469
00:36:58,755 --> 00:37:01,090
That price on my head,
was that dead or alive?

470
00:37:01,257 --> 00:37:03,801
Don't remember.
Let's see if he starts shooting.

471
00:37:03,968 --> 00:37:07,597
Run interference. I'll meet you downstairs
in the bar in, say, uh, half an hour?

472
00:37:07,764 --> 00:37:09,766
- Back here?
- This is the last place they'd suspect.

473
00:37:09,933 --> 00:37:11,226
[CHUCKLES]

474
00:37:12,310 --> 00:37:13,645
All right.

475
00:37:14,812 --> 00:37:16,648
Freddy. Freddy Simmonds.

476
00:37:16,814 --> 00:37:18,274
My God, it's you, isn't it?

477
00:37:19,776 --> 00:37:20,944
[GRUNTS]

478
00:37:21,819 --> 00:37:22,904
No, it isn't you.

479
00:37:23,071 --> 00:37:24,489
Not dreaming now, are you?

480
00:37:24,656 --> 00:37:26,366
[GRUNTS THEN PEOPLE SHOUTING]

481
00:37:26,699 --> 00:37:28,284
MAN:
Get him!

482
00:37:43,800 --> 00:37:44,926
There!

483
00:38:03,152 --> 00:38:05,196
[WAITER SPEAKING
IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE]

484
00:38:05,446 --> 00:38:07,198
One <i>café.</i>

485
00:38:11,536 --> 00:38:12,704
Shh.

486
00:38:14,497 --> 00:38:16,291
[WAITER SHOUTING]

487
00:38:18,334 --> 00:38:19,794
One <i>café.</i>

488
00:38:20,837 --> 00:38:22,297
One <i>café.</i>

489
00:38:23,464 --> 00:38:24,632
[MEN SHOUTING]

490
00:38:41,941 --> 00:38:42,984
[HORN HONKS]

491
00:38:56,789 --> 00:38:57,999
[GRUNTING]

492
00:39:11,512 --> 00:39:12,847
[TIRES SCREECH]

493
00:39:16,893 --> 00:39:18,353
Care for a lift, Mr. Cobb?

494
00:39:19,979 --> 00:39:24,067
- What are you doing in Mombasa?
- I'm here to protect my investment.

495
00:39:24,734 --> 00:39:26,110
[WHISTLE]

496
00:39:26,569 --> 00:39:29,530
Ah. So this is your idea
of losing a tail, huh?

497
00:39:29,697 --> 00:39:30,698
Different tail.

498
00:39:33,534 --> 00:39:35,036
[ARIADNE CLEARS THROAT]

499
00:39:39,415 --> 00:39:41,167
Cobb said you'd be back.

500
00:39:42,293 --> 00:39:45,838
- I tried not to come, but...
- But there's nothing quite like it.

501
00:39:46,005 --> 00:39:47,673
It's just...

502
00:39:49,175 --> 00:39:50,510
...pure creation.

503
00:39:50,676 --> 00:39:53,179
Shall we take a look
at some paradoxical architecture?

504
00:39:53,346 --> 00:39:55,348
ARTHUR: You're gonna have to master
a few tricks...

505
00:39:55,515 --> 00:39:59,060
...if you're gonna build three complete
dream levels. Excuse me.

506
00:39:59,227 --> 00:40:00,645
ARIADNE:
What kind of tricks?

507
00:40:00,812 --> 00:40:04,315
In a dream, you can cheat architecture
into impossible shapes.

508
00:40:04,482 --> 00:40:08,403
That lets you create closed loops,
like the Penrose Steps.

509
00:40:08,569 --> 00:40:09,737
The infinite staircase.

510
00:40:13,950 --> 00:40:15,409
See?

511
00:40:19,580 --> 00:40:20,623
Paradox.

512
00:40:20,790 --> 00:40:22,208
So a closed loop like that...

513
00:40:22,375 --> 00:40:25,461
...will help you disguise the boundaries
of the dream you create.

514
00:40:25,628 --> 00:40:27,588
But how big do these levels have to be?

515
00:40:27,755 --> 00:40:30,925
It could be anything from
the floor of a building to an entire city.

516
00:40:31,092 --> 00:40:34,512
They have to be complicated enough
that we can hide from the projections.

517
00:40:34,679 --> 00:40:36,097
- A maze?
- Right, a maze.

518
00:40:36,264 --> 00:40:37,598
And the better the maze...

519
00:40:37,765 --> 00:40:40,434
Then the longer we have
before the projections catch us?

520
00:40:40,601 --> 00:40:41,894
Exactly.

521
00:40:42,562 --> 00:40:45,773
- My subconscious seems polite enough.
- Ha, ha. You wait, they'll turn ugly.

522
00:40:47,859 --> 00:40:49,277
No one likes to feel someone else
messing around in their mind.

523
00:40:49,443 --> 00:40:51,529
Cobb can't build anymore, can he?

524
00:40:53,364 --> 00:40:55,199
I don't know if he can't, but he won't.

525
00:40:55,366 --> 00:40:57,785
He thinks it's safer
if he doesn't know the layouts.

526
00:40:57,952 --> 00:40:59,036
Why?

527
00:40:59,203 --> 00:41:01,455
He won't tell me. But I think it's Mal.

528
00:41:01,873 --> 00:41:04,167
- His ex-wife?
- No, not his ex.

529
00:41:04,333 --> 00:41:07,503
- They're still together?
- No.

530
00:41:08,754 --> 00:41:10,256
No, she's dead.

531
00:41:12,216 --> 00:41:14,969
What you see in there
is just his projection of her.

532
00:41:18,472 --> 00:41:19,974
What was she like in real life?

533
00:41:20,641 --> 00:41:22,018
She was lovely.

534
00:41:25,855 --> 00:41:28,232
YUSUF: <i>You are seeking a chemist?</i>
COBB: <i>Yes.</i>

535
00:41:28,399 --> 00:41:30,526
To formulate compounds for a job?

536
00:41:31,194 --> 00:41:33,070
And to go into the field with us.

537
00:41:33,237 --> 00:41:35,489
No, I rarely go into the field, Mr. Cobb.

538
00:41:35,656 --> 00:41:39,368
Well, we'd need you there to tailor
compounds specific to our needs.

539
00:41:39,535 --> 00:41:41,662
- Which are?
- Great depth.

540
00:41:41,829 --> 00:41:45,082
A dream within a dream? Two levels?

541
00:41:47,835 --> 00:41:48,878
Three.

542
00:41:49,045 --> 00:41:52,632
Not possible. That many dreams
within dreams is too unstable.

543
00:41:52,798 --> 00:41:56,761
It is possible.
You just have to add a sedative.

544
00:41:56,928 --> 00:41:59,597
A powerful sedative.

545
00:42:01,390 --> 00:42:03,643
- How many team members?
- Five.

546
00:42:03,809 --> 00:42:05,519
SAITO:
Six.

547
00:42:06,395 --> 00:42:10,441
The only way to know you've done the job
is if I go in with you.

548
00:42:10,608 --> 00:42:13,694
There's no room for tourists
on a job like this, Mr. Saito.

549
00:42:13,861 --> 00:42:16,239
This time, it seems there is.

550
00:42:17,198 --> 00:42:20,910
This, I think, is a good place to start.
I use it every day.

551
00:42:21,077 --> 00:42:23,204
COBB: What for?
- Here, I'll show you.

552
00:42:27,541 --> 00:42:29,252
Perhaps you will not want to see.

553
00:42:32,213 --> 00:42:33,798
After you.

554
00:42:34,006 --> 00:42:35,675
[MACHINE HISSING]

555
00:42:43,099 --> 00:42:46,102
EAMES:
Ten. Twelve. All connected. Bloody hell.

556
00:42:46,269 --> 00:42:48,062
They come every day to share the dream.

557
00:42:51,065 --> 00:42:53,651
You see? Very stable.

558
00:42:59,365 --> 00:43:03,411
- How long do they dream for?
YUSUF: Three, four hours, each day.

559
00:43:03,703 --> 00:43:04,745
In dream time?

560
00:43:04,912 --> 00:43:08,582
With this compound? About 40 hours,
each and every day.

561
00:43:08,749 --> 00:43:12,044
- Why do they do it?
YUSUF: Tell him, Mr. Cobb.

562
00:43:13,587 --> 00:43:16,007
After a while, it becomes
the only way you can dream.

563
00:43:16,173 --> 00:43:18,092
Do you still dream, Mr. Cobb?

564
00:43:18,759 --> 00:43:22,138
- They come here every day to sleep?
MAN: No.

565
00:43:23,889 --> 00:43:26,600
They come to be woken up.

566
00:43:27,601 --> 00:43:30,896
The dream has become their reality.

567
00:43:31,814 --> 00:43:34,191
Who are you to say otherwise, sir?

568
00:43:36,485 --> 00:43:38,112
Let's see what you can do.

569
00:43:45,619 --> 00:43:47,038
[METAL GROANING]

570
00:43:49,123 --> 00:43:50,708
You know how to find me.

571
00:43:53,294 --> 00:43:55,296
You know what you have to do.

572
00:44:00,551 --> 00:44:01,635
Sharp, no?

573
00:44:17,193 --> 00:44:20,029
SAITO:
Are you all right, Mr. Cobb?

574
00:44:20,571 --> 00:44:24,325
Yeah, yeah. Everything's just fine.

575
00:44:32,792 --> 00:44:36,504
Robert Fischer, heir to the Fischer Morrow
energy conglomerate.

576
00:44:36,670 --> 00:44:38,547
COBB:
What's your problem with this Mr. Fischer?

577
00:44:38,714 --> 00:44:40,341
That's not your concern.

578
00:44:40,716 --> 00:44:45,096
Mr. Saito, this isn't your typical
corporate espionage.

579
00:44:45,262 --> 00:44:46,847
You asked me for inception.

580
00:44:47,014 --> 00:44:50,393
I do hope you understand
the gravity of that request.

581
00:44:50,559 --> 00:44:54,522
Now, the seed that we plant
in this man's mind will grow into an idea.

582
00:44:54,688 --> 00:44:56,565
This idea will define him.

583
00:44:56,732 --> 00:44:58,567
It may come to change...

584
00:44:58,734 --> 00:45:01,028
Well, it may come to change
everything about him.

585
00:45:01,195 --> 00:45:05,533
We're the last company standing
between them and total energy dominance.

586
00:45:05,699 --> 00:45:07,785
And we can no longer compete.

587
00:45:07,952 --> 00:45:11,414
Soon, they'll control the energy supply
of half the world.

588
00:45:11,580 --> 00:45:13,958
In effect, they become
a new superpower.

589
00:45:15,376 --> 00:45:18,712
The world needs Robert Fischer
to change his mind.

590
00:45:18,879 --> 00:45:20,423
EAMES:
That's where we come in.

591
00:45:20,589 --> 00:45:23,884
How is Robert Fischer's relationship
with his father?

592
00:45:24,051 --> 00:45:26,762
Rumor is the relationship
is quite complicated.

593
00:45:26,929 --> 00:45:29,432
Well, we can't work based
solely on rumor, can we?

594
00:45:29,598 --> 00:45:32,810
Can you get me access to this man here?
Browning.

595
00:45:32,977 --> 00:45:37,148
Fischer Senior's right-hand man.
Fischer Junior's godfather.

596
00:45:37,314 --> 00:45:40,568
It should be possible,
if you can get the right references.

597
00:45:40,734 --> 00:45:45,239
References are something
of a specialty for me, Mr. Saito.

598
00:45:45,865 --> 00:45:48,784
BROWNING: I'm not smelling
settlement here. Take them down.

599
00:45:48,951 --> 00:45:50,077
LAWYER:
Mr. Browning...

600
00:45:50,244 --> 00:45:54,081
...Maurice Fischer's policy
is always one of avoiding litigation.

601
00:45:56,625 --> 00:46:01,088
Well, shall we voice your concerns
with Maurice directly?

602
00:46:01,255 --> 00:46:02,673
Not sure that's necessary.

603
00:46:02,840 --> 00:46:05,509
No, no, no. I think we should.

604
00:46:26,780 --> 00:46:28,365
BROWNING:
How is he?

605
00:46:29,200 --> 00:46:31,952
I don't want to bother him
unnecessarily, but--

606
00:46:32,119 --> 00:46:37,625
MAURICE: Robert, I told you keep out
the damn-- Wait. So do it. Get--

607
00:46:37,875 --> 00:46:40,961
NURSE: Mr. Fischer.
MAURICE: Put it through.

608
00:46:41,337 --> 00:46:44,965
Never, never.
Never do the same as I asked.

609
00:46:46,258 --> 00:46:47,927
Leave that.

610
00:46:51,639 --> 00:46:52,973
Here.

611
00:46:55,267 --> 00:46:57,645
BROWNING:
Must be a cherished memory of his.

612
00:46:58,771 --> 00:47:03,317
I put it beside his bed.
He hasn't even noticed.

613
00:47:03,484 --> 00:47:05,152
Robert...

614
00:47:07,488 --> 00:47:09,907
...we need to talk about
a power of attorney.

615
00:47:10,074 --> 00:47:13,369
- I know this is hard, but it's imperative--
- Not now, Uncle Peter.

616
00:47:17,373 --> 00:47:19,166
EAMES:
<i>The vultures are circling.</i>

617
00:47:19,333 --> 00:47:23,754
<i>And the sicker Maurice Fischer becomes,
the more powerful Peter Browning becomes.</i>

618
00:47:23,921 --> 00:47:26,340
I've had ample opportunity
to observe Browning...

619
00:47:26,966 --> 00:47:31,345
<i>...and adopt his physical presence, study
his mannerisms, and so on and so forth.</i>

620
00:47:31,512 --> 00:47:35,349
So now in the first layer of the dream,
I can impersonate Browning.

621
00:47:35,516 --> 00:47:38,769
<i>And suggest concepts
to Fischer's conscious mind.</i>

622
00:47:38,936 --> 00:47:40,771
Then, when we take him
a level deeper...

623
00:47:40,938 --> 00:47:44,441
...his own projection of Browning
should feed that right back to him.

624
00:47:44,608 --> 00:47:46,527
So he gives himself the idea.

625
00:47:46,694 --> 00:47:50,948
Precisely. That's the only way it will stick.
It has to seem self-generated.

626
00:47:51,115 --> 00:47:54,535
Eames, I am impressed.

627
00:47:54,702 --> 00:47:58,247
Your condescension, as always,
is much appreciated, Arthur, thank you.

628
00:48:10,551 --> 00:48:11,760
[DOOR OPENS]

629
00:48:19,602 --> 00:48:21,604
Were you going under on your own?

630
00:48:21,770 --> 00:48:25,274
No, no, I was just, uh, running
some experiments.

631
00:48:25,441 --> 00:48:27,401
I didn't realize anyone was here, so...

632
00:48:27,568 --> 00:48:30,237
Yeah, I was just--
I was working on my totem, actually.

633
00:48:30,404 --> 00:48:31,572
Here, let me take a look.

634
00:48:33,741 --> 00:48:35,075
So you're learning, huh?

635
00:48:35,242 --> 00:48:38,746
An elegant solution
for keeping track of reality.

636
00:48:39,371 --> 00:48:41,749
- Was it your idea?
- No, it was, uh...

637
00:48:41,999 --> 00:48:44,084
It was Mai's, actually. This...

638
00:48:44,752 --> 00:48:48,547
This one was hers. She would spin it
in the dream and it would never topple.

639
00:48:48,756 --> 00:48:51,634
Just spin and spin.

640
00:48:53,177 --> 00:48:55,095
Arthur told me she passed away.

641
00:48:57,014 --> 00:48:59,099
How are the mazes coming along?

642
00:48:59,266 --> 00:49:02,144
Each level relates to the part
of the subject's subconscious...

643
00:49:02,311 --> 00:49:03,771
...that we are trying to access.

644
00:49:03,937 --> 00:49:09,401
So I'm making the bottom level a hospital,
so Fischer will bring his father.

645
00:49:09,777 --> 00:49:12,529
You know, I-- Actually, I have a question
about this layout.

646
00:49:12,696 --> 00:49:17,451
No, no, no. Don't show me specifics.
Only the dreamer should know the layout.

647
00:49:17,618 --> 00:49:18,786
Why is that so important?

648
00:49:18,952 --> 00:49:20,954
In case one of us brings in
our projections.

649
00:49:21,121 --> 00:49:23,457
We don't want them knowing
the details of the maze.

650
00:49:24,833 --> 00:49:27,294
You mean in case you bring Mal in.

651
00:49:27,461 --> 00:49:29,880
You can't keep her out, can you?

652
00:49:30,798 --> 00:49:32,299
- Right.
- You can't build...

653
00:49:32,466 --> 00:49:35,803
...because if you know the maze,
then she knows it.

654
00:49:35,969 --> 00:49:38,138
Well, she'd sabotage
the whole operation.

655
00:49:38,305 --> 00:49:41,141
- Cobb, do the others know?
- No. No, they don't.

656
00:49:41,308 --> 00:49:43,519
You've gotta warn them
if this is getting worse.

657
00:49:43,686 --> 00:49:45,646
No one said it's getting worse.

658
00:49:46,814 --> 00:49:50,150
I need to get home.
That's all I care about right now.

659
00:49:50,984 --> 00:49:53,070
Why can't you go home?

660
00:49:56,156 --> 00:49:58,200
Because they think I killed her.

661
00:50:01,453 --> 00:50:03,706
- Thank you.
- For what?

662
00:50:03,914 --> 00:50:06,166
For not asking whether I did.

663
00:50:06,333 --> 00:50:08,502
"I will split up my father's empire."

664
00:50:08,669 --> 00:50:12,881
Now, this is obviously an idea that
Robert himself would choose to reject...

665
00:50:13,048 --> 00:50:15,843
...which is why we need to plant it deep
in his subconscious.

666
00:50:16,009 --> 00:50:19,555
The subconscious is motivated
by emotion, right? Not reason.

667
00:50:19,722 --> 00:50:23,517
We need to find a way to translate this
into an emotional concept.

668
00:50:23,684 --> 00:50:26,103
How do you translate business strategy
into emotion?

669
00:50:26,270 --> 00:50:29,857
That's what we're here to figure out.
Robert's relationship with his father...

670
00:50:30,023 --> 00:50:32,776
- ...is stressed, to say the least.
- Can we run with that?

671
00:50:32,943 --> 00:50:36,530
We suggest breaking up his father's
company as a "screw you" to the old man.

672
00:50:36,697 --> 00:50:40,200
No, because I think positive emotion
trumps negative emotion every time.

673
00:50:40,576 --> 00:50:44,288
We all yearn for reconciliation,
for catharsis.

674
00:50:44,455 --> 00:50:48,208
We need Robert Fischer to have
a positive emotional reaction to all this.

675
00:50:48,375 --> 00:50:50,210
All right, well, try this. Um...

676
00:50:50,377 --> 00:50:55,132
"My father accepts that I want to create
for myself, not follow in his footsteps."

677
00:50:55,549 --> 00:50:56,592
That might work.

678
00:50:56,759 --> 00:50:59,928
Might? We're gonna need to do
a little better than "might."

679
00:51:00,095 --> 00:51:02,055
Thank you for your contribution, Arthur.

680
00:51:02,222 --> 00:51:05,184
Forgive me for wanting
a little specificity, Eames.

681
00:51:05,392 --> 00:51:06,477
Specificity?

682
00:51:06,643 --> 00:51:08,479
Inception's not about being specific.

683
00:51:08,645 --> 00:51:12,608
When we get inside his mind, we're
gonna have to work with what we find.

684
00:51:13,901 --> 00:51:16,904
EAMES: On the top level, we open up
his relationship with his father...

685
00:51:17,070 --> 00:51:20,157
...and say, "I will not follow
in my father's footsteps."

686
00:51:20,324 --> 00:51:24,995
Then the next level down, we feed him,
"I will create something for myself."

687
00:51:25,162 --> 00:51:28,499
Then, by the time we hit the bottom level,
we bring out the big guns.

688
00:51:28,665 --> 00:51:31,084
- "My father doesn't want me to be him."
EAMES: Exactly.

689
00:51:31,251 --> 00:51:32,252
[MACHINE HISSING]

690
00:51:32,419 --> 00:51:36,423
Three layers down, the dreams are gonna
collapse with the slightest disturbance.

691
00:51:36,590 --> 00:51:37,758
Sedation.

692
00:51:37,925 --> 00:51:40,761
For sleep stable enough to create
three layers of dreaming...

693
00:51:40,928 --> 00:51:44,848
...we'll have to combine it with
an extremely powerful sedative.

694
00:51:45,182 --> 00:51:46,767
[MACHINE HISSING]

695
00:51:56,276 --> 00:51:57,361
Good night.

696
00:51:57,945 --> 00:52:00,155
YUSUF: <i>The compound we'll be using
to share the dream...</i>

697
00:52:00,322 --> 00:52:02,282
<i>...creates a clear connection
between dreamers...</i>

698
00:52:02,449 --> 00:52:06,662
- ...whilst accelerating brain function.
- In other words, more time on each level.

699
00:52:06,829 --> 00:52:09,957
Brain function in the dream will be
about 20 times normal.

700
00:52:10,165 --> 00:52:13,669
And when you enter a dream within
that dream, the effect is compounded.

701
00:52:13,836 --> 00:52:15,963
It's three dreams,
that's 10 hours times 20--

702
00:52:16,129 --> 00:52:19,007
Math was never my strong subject.
How much time is that?

703
00:52:19,174 --> 00:52:21,844
It's a week, the first level down.

704
00:52:22,553 --> 00:52:25,389
Six months the second level down,
and the third level--

705
00:52:25,556 --> 00:52:26,974
That's 10 years.

706
00:52:29,643 --> 00:52:31,812
Who'd wanna be stuck
in a dream for 10 years?

707
00:52:32,187 --> 00:52:33,355
Depends on the dream.

708
00:52:33,522 --> 00:52:35,816
So once we've made the plant,
how do we get out?

709
00:52:35,983 --> 00:52:39,319
I'm hoping you have something more
elegant than shooting me in the head.

710
00:52:39,486 --> 00:52:42,281
- A kick.
- What's a kick?

711
00:52:42,447 --> 00:52:45,158
EAMES:
This, Ariadne, would be a kick.

712
00:52:47,286 --> 00:52:49,997
It's that feeling of falling you get
that jolts you awake.

713
00:52:50,163 --> 00:52:53,375
- Snaps you out of the dream.
- We gonna feel a kick with this sedation?

714
00:52:53,542 --> 00:52:54,751
YUSUF:
That's the clever part.

715
00:52:54,918 --> 00:52:58,171
I customized the sedative to leave
inner-ear function unimpaired.

716
00:52:58,338 --> 00:53:03,176
That way, however deep the sleep,
the sleeper still feels falling.

717
00:53:03,343 --> 00:53:05,679
<i>Or tipping.</i>

718
00:53:06,346 --> 00:53:10,517
The trick is to synchronize a kick
that can penetrate all three levels.

719
00:53:11,018 --> 00:53:14,563
We could use a musical countdown
to synchronize the different kicks.

720
00:53:14,730 --> 00:53:17,065
["NON, JE NE REGRETTE RIEN" PLAYING]

721
00:53:22,446 --> 00:53:25,949
EAMES: <i>He hasn't got any surgery
scheduled, no dental, nothing.</i>

722
00:53:26,116 --> 00:53:28,118
COBB: <i>Wasn't he supposed to have
a knee operation?</i>

723
00:53:28,285 --> 00:53:31,538
Nothing. Nothing that they'll
put him under for, anyway. And we need--

724
00:53:31,705 --> 00:53:33,624
We need at least a good 10 hours.

725
00:53:33,790 --> 00:53:35,125
Sydney to Los Angeles.

726
00:53:36,460 --> 00:53:40,714
One of the longest flights in the world.
He makes it every two weeks.

727
00:53:40,881 --> 00:53:43,050
He must be flying private, then.

728
00:53:43,550 --> 00:53:46,720
SAITO: Not if there were unexpected
maintenance with his plane.

729
00:53:46,887 --> 00:53:49,139
ARTHUR: It would have to be a 747.
- Why's that?

730
00:53:49,306 --> 00:53:50,766
On a 747, the pilot's up top...

731
00:53:50,933 --> 00:53:54,144
...the first-class cabin's in the nose,
so no one would walk through.

732
00:53:54,311 --> 00:53:57,814
You'd have to buy out the entire cabin
and the first-class flight attendant.

733
00:53:57,981 --> 00:53:59,566
I bought the airline.

734
00:54:01,109 --> 00:54:02,903
It seemed neater.

735
00:54:04,529 --> 00:54:07,324
Well, looks like we have our 10 hours.

736
00:54:07,991 --> 00:54:11,119
Ariadne? Terrific work, by the way.

737
00:54:13,914 --> 00:54:15,582
[MACHINE HISSING]

738
00:54:59,126 --> 00:55:01,294
MAL:
You know how to find me.

739
00:55:02,796 --> 00:55:04,214
You know what you have to do.

740
00:55:08,051 --> 00:55:11,513
You remember when you asked me
to marry you?

741
00:55:12,389 --> 00:55:14,433
Of course I do.

742
00:55:15,517 --> 00:55:18,020
You said you had a dream.

743
00:55:20,981 --> 00:55:23,734
That we'd grow old together.

744
00:55:25,152 --> 00:55:26,862
And we can.

745
00:55:33,535 --> 00:55:35,495
COBB:
You shouldn't be here.

746
00:55:39,249 --> 00:55:42,711
Just wanted to see what kind of tests
you're doing on your own every night.

747
00:55:42,878 --> 00:55:46,089
- This has nothing to do with you.
- This has everything to do with me.

748
00:55:46,256 --> 00:55:52,054
- You've asked me to share dreams with you.
- Not these. These are my dreams.

749
00:56:02,939 --> 00:56:05,358
ARIADNE:
Why do you do this to yourself?

750
00:56:05,525 --> 00:56:08,445
It's the only way I can still dream.

751
00:56:10,197 --> 00:56:12,407
Why is it so important to dream?

752
00:56:13,325 --> 00:56:15,577
In my dreams, we're still together.

753
00:56:29,925 --> 00:56:31,676
These aren't just dreams.

754
00:56:33,470 --> 00:56:36,515
These are memories.
And you said never to use memories.

755
00:56:36,681 --> 00:56:37,724
I know I did.

756
00:56:38,391 --> 00:56:42,896
You're trying to keep her alive.
You can't let her go.

757
00:56:43,063 --> 00:56:46,066
You don't understand.
These are moments I regret.

758
00:56:46,233 --> 00:56:48,819
They're memories that I have to change.

759
00:56:50,070 --> 00:56:52,531
Well, what's down there that you regret?

760
00:56:52,906 --> 00:56:56,076
Listen, there's only one thing
you need to understand about me.

761
00:57:04,543 --> 00:57:06,128
ARIADNE:
This is your house?

762
00:57:06,795 --> 00:57:09,256
Mine and Mai's, yes.

763
00:57:09,422 --> 00:57:11,133
Where is she?

764
00:57:11,800 --> 00:57:13,802
She's already gone.

765
00:57:18,557 --> 00:57:20,100
That's my son, James.

766
00:57:20,267 --> 00:57:23,353
He's digging for something,
maybe a worm.

767
00:57:23,520 --> 00:57:24,563
[JAMES GIGGLING]

768
00:57:24,729 --> 00:57:25,856
That's Phillipa.

769
00:57:26,398 --> 00:57:28,275
I thought about calling out to them...

770
00:57:28,441 --> 00:57:31,194
...so they'd turn and smile
and I could see...

771
00:57:31,903 --> 00:57:37,033
...those beautiful faces of theirs,
but it's all too late.

772
00:57:37,200 --> 00:57:40,036
MAN:
Right now or never, Cobb.

773
00:57:44,499 --> 00:57:46,293
COBB:
Then I start to panic.

774
00:57:46,459 --> 00:57:49,171
I realize I'm gonna regret this moment...

775
00:57:50,005 --> 00:57:52,340
...that I need to see their faces
one last time.

776
00:57:52,507 --> 00:57:55,010
WOMAN:
James! Phillipa! Come on in!

777
00:57:55,177 --> 00:57:57,220
COBB:
But the moment's past.

778
00:57:59,431 --> 00:58:02,809
And whatever I do,
I can't change this moment.

779
00:58:03,685 --> 00:58:05,854
As I'm about to call out to them...

780
00:58:06,897 --> 00:58:07,981
...they run away.

781
00:58:10,734 --> 00:58:13,987
If I'm ever gonna see their faces again,
I've gotta get back home.

782
00:58:15,030 --> 00:58:16,531
The real world.

783
00:58:22,162 --> 00:58:23,830
[TRAIN APPROACHING]

784
00:58:45,560 --> 00:58:47,562
[SIREN WAILING IN THE DISTANCE]

785
00:58:58,114 --> 00:58:59,574
What are you doing here?

786
00:59:00,408 --> 00:59:01,534
My name is--

787
00:59:01,701 --> 00:59:04,996
I know who you are.
What are you doing here?

788
00:59:13,171 --> 00:59:15,590
I'm just trying to understand.

789
00:59:15,757 --> 00:59:17,884
How could you understand?

790
00:59:19,094 --> 00:59:21,221
Do you know what it is to be a lover?

791
00:59:23,223 --> 00:59:25,392
To be half of a whole?

792
00:59:26,601 --> 00:59:28,186
No.

793
00:59:29,646 --> 00:59:31,648
I'll tell you a riddle.

794
00:59:33,108 --> 00:59:35,402
You're waiting for a train.

795
00:59:36,444 --> 00:59:39,614
A train that will take you far away.

796
00:59:40,991 --> 00:59:43,952
You know where you hope
this train will take you...

797
00:59:44,995 --> 00:59:47,247
...but you don't know for sure.

798
00:59:47,414 --> 00:59:49,332
But it doesn't matter.

799
00:59:50,292 --> 00:59:53,712
How can it not matter to you
where that train will take you?

800
00:59:53,878 --> 00:59:55,630
COBB:
Because you'll be together.

801
00:59:56,673 --> 00:59:59,759
MAL: How could you bring her here, Dom?
- What is this place?

802
01:00:00,176 --> 01:00:03,346
This is a hotel suite where we
used to spend our anniversary.

803
01:00:03,513 --> 01:00:04,848
What happened here?

804
01:00:06,766 --> 01:00:09,602
You promised!
You promised we'd be together!

805
01:00:10,020 --> 01:00:12,105
Please, I need you to stay here
just for now!

806
01:00:12,272 --> 01:00:15,483
You said we'd be together!
You said we'd grow old together!

807
01:00:15,650 --> 01:00:18,111
I'll come back for you. I promise.

808
01:00:18,278 --> 01:00:19,779
[YELLING]

809
01:00:36,338 --> 01:00:41,718
Do you think you can just build
a prison of memories to lock her in?

810
01:00:42,344 --> 01:00:44,721
Do you really think that
that's gonna contain her?

811
01:00:45,638 --> 01:00:46,890
SAITO:
It's time.

812
01:00:48,141 --> 01:00:50,477
Maurice Fischer just died in Sydney.

813
01:00:50,643 --> 01:00:52,812
- When's the funeral?
- Thursday. In Los Angeles.

814
01:00:52,979 --> 01:00:56,232
Robert should accompany the body
no later than Tuesday. We should move.

815
01:00:56,399 --> 01:00:57,859
All right.

816
01:00:58,485 --> 01:01:01,488
- Cobb, I'm coming with you.
- I promised Miles. No.

817
01:01:01,654 --> 01:01:04,824
The team needs someone who
understands what you're struggling with.

818
01:01:07,369 --> 01:01:09,537
And it doesn't have to be me...

819
01:01:09,704 --> 01:01:12,999
...but then you have to show Arthur
what I just saw.

820
01:01:18,797 --> 01:01:20,507
Get us another seat on the plane.

821
01:01:24,552 --> 01:01:27,597
COBB: If I get on this plane
and you don't honor our agreement...

822
01:01:27,764 --> 01:01:31,434
...when we land,
I go to jail for the rest of my life.

823
01:01:32,102 --> 01:01:34,312
Complete the job en route...

824
01:01:34,479 --> 01:01:36,940
...I make one phone call from the plane...

825
01:01:37,107 --> 01:01:40,026
...you have no trouble
getting through Immigration.

826
01:01:53,748 --> 01:01:56,251
- I'm sorry.
- Oh, yeah, absolutely.

827
01:01:56,418 --> 01:01:58,002
Thank you.

828
01:02:31,578 --> 01:02:34,914
Excuse me, I think this is yours?
You must have dropped it.

829
01:02:35,081 --> 01:02:36,958
FLIGHT ATTENDANT:
Would you care for a drink?

830
01:02:37,125 --> 01:02:38,418
Oh. Water, please.

831
01:02:38,585 --> 01:02:40,920
Oh, um, same, please.

832
01:02:41,880 --> 01:02:43,173
Um...

833
01:02:43,923 --> 01:02:45,008
Thank you.

834
01:02:45,175 --> 01:02:47,051
You know, I couldn't help but notice...

835
01:02:47,218 --> 01:02:50,805
...but you wouldn't happen to be related
to the Maurice Fischer, would you?

836
01:02:50,972 --> 01:02:52,474
Yes, he, um...

837
01:02:53,433 --> 01:02:55,435
He was my father.

838
01:02:55,852 --> 01:02:59,898
Well, he was a very inspiring figure.
I'm sorry for your loss.

839
01:03:02,859 --> 01:03:04,527
- Here you go.
- Thank you.

840
01:03:04,694 --> 01:03:06,029
COBB:
Hey.

841
01:03:06,446 --> 01:03:08,072
To your father.

842
01:03:08,656 --> 01:03:10,909
May he rest in peace, huh?

843
01:04:02,377 --> 01:04:03,878
[MACHINE HISSING]

844
01:04:14,681 --> 01:04:16,015
[HORN HONKS]

845
01:04:19,352 --> 01:04:21,688
- Couldn't have peed before you went under?
- Sorry.

846
01:04:21,854 --> 01:04:23,898
Bit too much free champagne
before takeoff?

847
01:04:24,065 --> 01:04:25,191
Ha, ha, bloody ha.

848
01:04:25,358 --> 01:04:28,319
We know he's gonna be looking for a taxi
in this weather.

849
01:04:39,998 --> 01:04:44,043
- Asshole! Hey, man, why don't you try--?
- Walk away.

850
01:04:56,139 --> 01:04:58,474
[MAN SINGING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE
ON RADIO]

851
01:04:58,641 --> 01:04:59,726
[TURNS OFF RADIO]

852
01:04:59,892 --> 01:05:01,311
Just have him ca-- I gotta go.

853
01:05:01,477 --> 01:05:03,605
All right. Taxi. Thank you.

854
01:05:06,566 --> 01:05:09,402
All right, Third and Market. Snappy.

855
01:05:09,986 --> 01:05:12,322
- What are you doing?
- Sorry, I thought it was free.

856
01:05:12,488 --> 01:05:13,990
- It's not.
- Maybe we could share.

857
01:05:14,157 --> 01:05:16,993
Maybe not.
Can you pull over and get this--?

858
01:05:19,954 --> 01:05:21,289
Great.

859
01:05:26,252 --> 01:05:27,712
Come on.

860
01:05:38,806 --> 01:05:41,434
There's $500 in there.
The wallet's worth more than that.

861
01:05:41,601 --> 01:05:43,311
You might at least
drop me at my stop.

862
01:05:43,478 --> 01:05:44,604
I'm afraid that it doesn't--

863
01:05:44,771 --> 01:05:46,314
[GUNFIRE THEN EAMES GRUNTS]

864
01:05:47,607 --> 01:05:49,275
[TIRES SCREECHING]

865
01:05:54,280 --> 01:05:56,115
- Cover him!
EAMES:Down! Down now!

866
01:05:56,282 --> 01:05:57,909
What the hell is going on?

867
01:06:01,621 --> 01:06:03,873
ARIADNE:
This wasn't in the design.

868
01:06:04,791 --> 01:06:06,167
Cobb?

869
01:06:06,417 --> 01:06:07,460
Cobb?

870
01:06:12,924 --> 01:06:14,467
[GUNFIRE]

871
01:06:51,129 --> 01:06:52,255
[YELLS]

872
01:06:58,344 --> 01:06:59,554
Get him!

873
01:07:06,227 --> 01:07:07,687
Are you all right?

874
01:07:07,854 --> 01:07:10,022
Yeah, I'm okay. I'm okay.

875
01:07:10,189 --> 01:07:12,108
Fischer's okay, unless he gets carsick.

876
01:07:12,275 --> 01:07:13,526
Saito?

877
01:07:32,420 --> 01:07:34,547
COBB:
Get Fischer in the back room now.

878
01:07:34,881 --> 01:07:37,550
- Get him in the back room. Move.
ARTHUR: What the hell happened?

879
01:07:38,009 --> 01:07:39,469
COBB:
Has he been shot? Is he dying?

880
01:07:39,635 --> 01:07:41,387
ARTHUR: I don't know.
- Jesus Christ.

881
01:07:41,554 --> 01:07:43,973
ARTHUR: What happened to you?
- Blocked by a freight train.

882
01:07:44,140 --> 01:07:46,100
Why put a train
in a downtown intersection?

883
01:07:46,267 --> 01:07:47,894
- I didn't.
ARTHUR: Where'd it come from?

884
01:07:48,060 --> 01:07:49,729
Why the hell were we ambushed?

885
01:07:49,896 --> 01:07:52,315
Those were not normal projections.
They'd been trained, for God's sakes.

886
01:07:54,400 --> 01:07:56,027
- How could he be trained?
- Fischer's had an extractor...

887
01:07:56,194 --> 01:08:00,406
...teach his subconscious to defend itself,
so his subconscious is militarized.

888
01:08:00,573 --> 01:08:02,200
It should've shown in the research.

889
01:08:02,366 --> 01:08:04,160
COBB: Why the hell didn't it?
- Calm down.

890
01:08:04,327 --> 01:08:05,661
COBB:
Don't tell me to calm down!

891
01:08:05,828 --> 01:08:08,748
This was your job, goddamn it!
This was your responsibility!

892
01:08:08,915 --> 01:08:11,000
You were meant to check
Fischer's background!

893
01:08:11,167 --> 01:08:14,504
- We are not prepared for this!
- We have dealt with sub-security before!

894
01:08:14,670 --> 01:08:16,756
We'll be more careful
and we're gonna be fine!

895
01:08:16,923 --> 01:08:18,758
This was not a part of the plan!
He's dying.

896
01:08:18,925 --> 01:08:19,967
Put him out his misery.

897
01:08:20,134 --> 01:08:22,970
- No, don't do that! Don't do that.
- Cobb, hey, hey.

898
01:08:23,137 --> 01:08:25,264
He's in agony. I'm waking him up.

899
01:08:25,431 --> 01:08:27,308
No. It won't wake him up.

900
01:08:27,475 --> 01:08:31,103
What do you mean, it won't wake him up?
When we die in a dream, we wake up.

901
01:08:31,270 --> 01:08:32,522
Not from this.

902
01:08:32,688 --> 01:08:35,233
We're too heavily sedated
to wake up that way.

903
01:08:35,816 --> 01:08:37,527
Right. So, what happens when we die?

904
01:08:37,693 --> 01:08:39,529
- We drop into limbo.
- Are you serious?

905
01:08:39,695 --> 01:08:42,240
- Limbo?
ARTHUR: Unconstructed dream space.

906
01:08:42,406 --> 01:08:45,660
ARIADNE: What the hell is down there?
- Just raw, infinite subconscious.

907
01:08:45,826 --> 01:08:49,038
Nothing is down there, except for
what might have been left behind...

908
01:08:49,205 --> 01:08:52,124
...by anyone sharing the dream
who's been trapped there before.

909
01:08:52,291 --> 01:08:54,168
Which, in our case, is just you.

910
01:08:54,335 --> 01:08:57,046
- How long could we be stuck?
YUSUF: Can't even think about...

911
01:08:57,213 --> 01:08:59,632
- ...trying to escape until the sedation--
- How long?

912
01:08:59,799 --> 01:09:03,010
Decades. It could be infinite.
Ask him, he's the one who's been there.

913
01:09:03,177 --> 01:09:04,387
ARTHUR:
Let's get him upstairs.

914
01:09:04,554 --> 01:09:05,846
[GROANING]

915
01:09:06,013 --> 01:09:07,473
Great.

916
01:09:08,474 --> 01:09:09,976
Thank you.

917
01:09:10,643 --> 01:09:13,896
So now we're trapped in Fischer's mind,
battling his own private army.

918
01:09:14,063 --> 01:09:15,523
And if we get killed...

919
01:09:15,690 --> 01:09:18,943
...we'll be lost in limbo till our brains
turn to scrambled egg, hmm?

920
01:09:25,825 --> 01:09:27,660
ARTHUR:
Someone got first aid?

921
01:09:29,912 --> 01:09:33,374
- You knew these risks and didn't tell us?
- There weren't meant to be risks.

922
01:09:33,541 --> 01:09:36,502
- I didn't know we'd be dealing with gunfire.
- You had no right.

923
01:09:36,669 --> 01:09:39,005
This was the only way
to go three layers deep.

924
01:09:39,171 --> 01:09:41,173
You knew about this
and went along with it?

925
01:09:41,340 --> 01:09:42,383
I trusted him.

926
01:09:42,550 --> 01:09:44,468
When? When he promised you
half his share?

927
01:09:44,635 --> 01:09:47,888
No. His whole share.
Besides, he said he'd done it before.

928
01:09:48,055 --> 01:09:50,141
What, with Mal?
Because that worked so good?

929
01:09:50,308 --> 01:09:53,728
That has nothing to do with it.
I did what I had to to get to my children.

930
01:09:53,894 --> 01:09:55,813
You led us into a war zone
with no way out?

931
01:09:55,980 --> 01:10:00,026
There is a way out. We continue on with
the job, and we do it as fast as possible...

932
01:10:00,192 --> 01:10:02,528
...and we get out using the kick,
just like before.

933
01:10:02,695 --> 01:10:05,156
Forget it. We go any deeper,
we just raise the stakes.

934
01:10:05,323 --> 01:10:07,199
I am sitting this one out on this level.

935
01:10:07,366 --> 01:10:09,785
COBB: Fischer's security
is surrounding this place.

936
01:10:09,952 --> 01:10:12,204
Ten hours of flight time
is a week at this level.

937
01:10:12,371 --> 01:10:16,125
That means each and every one of us
will be killed. That I can guarantee you.

938
01:10:16,709 --> 01:10:20,504
We have no other choice but to continue on
and do it as fast as possible.

939
01:10:22,089 --> 01:10:24,550
Downwards is the only way forwards.

940
01:10:26,093 --> 01:10:27,219
Get ready.

941
01:10:27,386 --> 01:10:29,555
You, come on. Let's go shake him up.

942
01:10:34,435 --> 01:10:37,480
I'm insured against kidnapping
for up to 10 million.

943
01:10:37,647 --> 01:10:40,566
- This should be very simple.
COBB: Shut up! It won't be.

944
01:10:40,733 --> 01:10:43,027
ARTHUR: In your father's office,
below the bookshelves...

945
01:10:43,194 --> 01:10:45,237
...is his personal safe.
We need the combination.

946
01:10:45,404 --> 01:10:46,447
[SIGHS]

947
01:10:46,614 --> 01:10:48,032
I don't know any safe.

948
01:10:48,240 --> 01:10:51,077
COBB: That doesn't mean
you don't know the combination.

949
01:10:52,745 --> 01:10:54,080
Tell us what it is.

950
01:10:54,914 --> 01:10:56,666
I don't know.

951
01:11:01,587 --> 01:11:03,756
We have it on good authority
you do know.

952
01:11:03,923 --> 01:11:06,258
Yeah? Whose authority?

953
01:11:07,760 --> 01:11:11,263
- Five hundred dollars, this cost.
EAMES: What's inside it?

954
01:11:11,514 --> 01:11:15,267
Cash, cards, ID. And this.

955
01:11:20,314 --> 01:11:21,816
Useful?

956
01:11:22,566 --> 01:11:23,609
Maybe.

957
01:11:24,068 --> 01:11:25,861
You're on. You've got an hour.

958
01:11:26,278 --> 01:11:27,279
An hour?

959
01:11:28,614 --> 01:11:30,866
I was supposed to have all night
to crack this.

960
01:11:31,033 --> 01:11:33,619
And Saito wasn't supposed
to be shot in the chest.

961
01:11:33,786 --> 01:11:36,956
You've got one hour,
now get us something useful, please.

962
01:11:37,832 --> 01:11:39,333
[SCREAMING]

963
01:11:39,500 --> 01:11:41,961
- What's that?
- Good authority.

964
01:11:46,173 --> 01:11:47,883
Uncle Peter.

965
01:11:48,592 --> 01:11:50,720
Just make them stop.

966
01:11:51,011 --> 01:11:53,347
- The combination.
- I don't know it.

967
01:11:53,514 --> 01:11:56,183
- Why does Browning say you do?
- I don't know.

968
01:11:56,350 --> 01:11:58,811
Just let me talk to him
and I'll find out.

969
01:12:01,188 --> 01:12:03,899
You have one hour. Start talking.

970
01:12:05,901 --> 01:12:07,111
FISCHER:
You all right?

971
01:12:07,278 --> 01:12:08,821
[GROANING]

972
01:12:08,988 --> 01:12:10,322
You okay?

973
01:12:13,993 --> 01:12:17,663
Those bastards have had at me
for two days.

974
01:12:18,330 --> 01:12:20,833
They have someone with access
to your father's office.

975
01:12:21,000 --> 01:12:22,835
- They're trying to open his safe.
- Yeah.

976
01:12:23,002 --> 01:12:25,755
They thought I'd know the combination,
but I don't know it.

977
01:12:25,921 --> 01:12:28,674
- Yeah, well, neither do I, so...
- What?

978
01:12:29,425 --> 01:12:32,678
Maurice told me that when he passed,
you were the only one able to open it.

979
01:12:32,845 --> 01:12:35,347
No, he never gave me any combination.

980
01:12:36,098 --> 01:12:40,227
Maybe he did. I mean, maybe you
just didn't know it was a combination.

981
01:12:40,394 --> 01:12:41,604
Well, what, then?

982
01:12:41,771 --> 01:12:44,315
I don't know, some meaningful
combination of numbers...

983
01:12:44,482 --> 01:12:48,402
...based on your experiences
with Maurice.

984
01:12:50,112 --> 01:12:54,033
We didn't have very many, uh,
meaningful experiences together.

985
01:12:54,784 --> 01:12:56,243
Perhaps after your mother died.

986
01:12:58,913 --> 01:13:01,624
After my mother died,
you know what he told me?

987
01:13:03,292 --> 01:13:08,255
"Robert,
there's really nothing to be said."

988
01:13:08,422 --> 01:13:10,758
Oh, well, he was bad with emotion.

989
01:13:10,925 --> 01:13:13,636
I was 11, Uncle Peter.

990
01:13:14,386 --> 01:13:17,640
- How's he doing?
- He's in a lot of pain.

991
01:13:17,807 --> 01:13:21,393
When we get down to the lower levels,
the pain will be less intense.

992
01:13:21,560 --> 01:13:22,812
And if he dies?

993
01:13:23,979 --> 01:13:25,439
Worst-case scenario?

994
01:13:25,606 --> 01:13:28,317
When he wakes up,
his mind is completely gone.

995
01:13:28,484 --> 01:13:32,571
Cobb, I'll still honor the arrangement.

996
01:13:32,738 --> 01:13:34,406
COBB:
I appreciate that, Saito.

997
01:13:34,573 --> 01:13:38,661
But when you wake up, you won't even
remember that we had an arrangement.

998
01:13:38,828 --> 01:13:40,871
Limbo is gonna become your reality.

999
01:13:41,038 --> 01:13:44,792
You're gonna be lost down there so long
that you're gonna become an old man.

1000
01:13:45,251 --> 01:13:46,585
Filled with regret?

1001
01:13:48,045 --> 01:13:49,129
Waiting to die alone.

1002
01:13:49,797 --> 01:13:50,923
No.

1003
01:13:51,465 --> 01:13:53,092
I'll come back.

1004
01:13:53,926 --> 01:13:56,595
And we'll be young men together again.

1005
01:14:02,560 --> 01:14:03,602
Breathe.

1006
01:14:03,769 --> 01:14:06,814
These people are gonna kill us
if we don't give them the combination.

1007
01:14:06,981 --> 01:14:08,899
- They just wanna ransom us.
- I heard them.

1008
01:14:09,066 --> 01:14:12,945
They're gonna lock us in that van,
and then drive it into the river.

1009
01:14:13,112 --> 01:14:14,613
All right. What is in the safe?

1010
01:14:15,698 --> 01:14:16,866
Something for you.

1011
01:14:17,491 --> 01:14:21,620
Maurice always said
it was his most precious gift to you.

1012
01:14:22,371 --> 01:14:25,457
- A will.
- Maurice's will is with Port and Dunn.

1013
01:14:25,624 --> 01:14:26,834
That's an alternate.

1014
01:14:27,001 --> 01:14:29,378
This would supersede the other
if you want it to.

1015
01:14:29,545 --> 01:14:32,172
It splits up the component businesses
of Fischer Morrow.

1016
01:14:32,339 --> 01:14:36,051
It'd be the end of the entire empire
as we know it.

1017
01:14:36,218 --> 01:14:38,679
Destroying my whole inheritance?

1018
01:14:40,347 --> 01:14:43,517
- Why would he suggest such a thing?
- I just don't know.

1019
01:14:46,395 --> 01:14:48,355
He loved you, Robert.

1020
01:14:48,939 --> 01:14:50,566
In his own way.

1021
01:14:50,733 --> 01:14:52,526
FISCHER:
In his own way.

1022
01:14:53,819 --> 01:14:55,362
At the end...

1023
01:14:56,488 --> 01:14:58,824
...he called me in to his deathbed.

1024
01:14:59,325 --> 01:15:01,160
He could barely speak.

1025
01:15:02,244 --> 01:15:06,498
But he took the trouble
to tell me one last thing.

1026
01:15:09,001 --> 01:15:10,628
He pulled me close.

1027
01:15:13,297 --> 01:15:15,382
And I could only make out...

1028
01:15:16,258 --> 01:15:17,676
...one word.

1029
01:15:21,388 --> 01:15:23,349
"Disappointed."

1030
01:15:27,519 --> 01:15:28,854
[SIGHS]

1031
01:15:29,021 --> 01:15:30,189
When were you in limbo?

1032
01:15:31,774 --> 01:15:35,277
You might have the rest of the team
convinced to carry on with this job.

1033
01:15:35,444 --> 01:15:37,821
- But they don't know the truth.
- Truth? What truth?

1034
01:15:38,030 --> 01:15:42,159
The truth that, at any minute, you might
bring a freight train through the wall.

1035
01:15:42,326 --> 01:15:46,372
The truth that Mal is bursting
through your subconscious.

1036
01:15:46,538 --> 01:15:48,958
And the truth that,
as we go deeper into Fischer...

1037
01:15:49,124 --> 01:15:50,918
...we're also going deeper into you.

1038
01:15:51,752 --> 01:15:55,047
And I'm not sure
we're gonna like what we find.

1039
01:15:59,051 --> 01:16:00,511
We were working together.

1040
01:16:00,678 --> 01:16:04,348
We were exploring the concept
of a dream within a dream.

1041
01:16:04,556 --> 01:16:05,808
I kept pushing things.

1042
01:16:05,975 --> 01:16:09,645
I wanted to go deeper and deeper.
I wanted to go further.

1043
01:16:10,145 --> 01:16:15,025
I just didn't understand the concept that
hours could turn into years down there...

1044
01:16:15,192 --> 01:16:17,903
...that we could get trapped so deep...

1045
01:16:18,737 --> 01:16:22,074
...that when we wound up on the shore
of our own subconscious...

1046
01:16:22,658 --> 01:16:25,202
<i>...we lost sight of what was real.</i>

1047
01:16:31,250 --> 01:16:33,836
We created.
We built the world for ourselves.

1048
01:16:35,462 --> 01:16:37,464
<i>We did that for years.</i>

1049
01:16:38,590 --> 01:16:40,592
<i>We built our own world.</i>

1050
01:16:41,927 --> 01:16:43,637
How long were you stuck there?

1051
01:16:44,805 --> 01:16:46,932
Something like 50 years.

1052
01:16:50,310 --> 01:16:51,478
Jesus.

1053
01:16:53,772 --> 01:16:55,107
How could you stand it?

1054
01:16:55,274 --> 01:16:58,027
It wasn't so bad at first,
feeling like gods.

1055
01:16:58,193 --> 01:17:01,238
<i>The problem was knowing
that none of it was real.</i>

1056
01:17:01,405 --> 01:17:05,784
Eventually, it just became impossible
for me to live like that.

1057
01:17:05,951 --> 01:17:07,369
And what about for her?

1058
01:17:10,831 --> 01:17:14,752
<i>She had locked something away,
something deep inside her.</i>

1059
01:17:16,128 --> 01:17:21,383
<i>A truth that she had once known,
but chose to forget.</i>

1060
01:17:24,303 --> 01:17:26,805
Limbo became her reality.

1061
01:17:28,140 --> 01:17:29,975
What happened when you woke up?

1062
01:17:30,476 --> 01:17:34,146
Well, to wake up from that
after years, after decades...

1063
01:17:35,230 --> 01:17:39,735
<i>...to become old souls thrown back
into youth like that?</i>

1064
01:17:40,652 --> 01:17:44,406
<i>I knew something was wrong with her.
She just wouldn't admit it.</i>

1065
01:17:45,783 --> 01:17:48,035
Eventually, she told me the truth.

1066
01:17:48,494 --> 01:17:51,830
<i>She was possessed by an idea.</i>

1067
01:17:51,997 --> 01:17:57,711
<i>This one very simple idea
that changed everything.</i>

1068
01:17:58,837 --> 01:18:01,340
That our world wasn't real.

1069
01:18:02,257 --> 01:18:06,512
That she needed to wake up
to come back to reality...

1070
01:18:07,054 --> 01:18:09,431
...that in order to get back home...

1071
01:18:10,682 --> 01:18:12,476
...we had to kill ourselves.

1072
01:18:19,108 --> 01:18:22,569
<i>-What about your children?
- She thought they were projections...</i>

1073
01:18:22,736 --> 01:18:25,739
<i>...that our real children were waiting
for us up there somewhere.</i>

1074
01:18:25,906 --> 01:18:27,616
MAL: I'm their mother!
COBB: Calm down.

1075
01:18:27,783 --> 01:18:29,034
I can tell the difference.

1076
01:18:29,201 --> 01:18:31,537
If this is my dream,
why can't I control this?

1077
01:18:31,703 --> 01:18:33,413
You don't know you're dreaming!

1078
01:18:33,580 --> 01:18:35,791
COBB: <i>She was certain
there was nothing I could do...</i>

1079
01:18:35,958 --> 01:18:39,795
...no matter how much I begged,
no matter how much I pleaded.

1080
01:18:42,756 --> 01:18:45,801
She wanted to do it,
but she could not do it alone.

1081
01:18:45,968 --> 01:18:52,474
<i>She loved me too much, so she came up
with a plan on our anniversary.</i>

1082
01:19:18,542 --> 01:19:21,336
- Sweetheart, what are you doing?
- Join me.

1083
01:19:21,503 --> 01:19:25,757
Just-- Just step back inside. All right?
Just step back inside now, come on.

1084
01:19:25,924 --> 01:19:30,387
No. I'm going to jump,
and you're coming with me.

1085
01:19:30,554 --> 01:19:32,264
No, I'm not.

1086
01:19:32,431 --> 01:19:34,516
Now, you listen to me.

1087
01:19:35,100 --> 01:19:39,354
If you jump, you're not gonna wake up,
remember? You're gonna die.

1088
01:19:39,521 --> 01:19:41,440
Now, just step back inside.

1089
01:19:41,607 --> 01:19:44,359
Come on. Step back inside
so we can talk about this.

1090
01:19:44,526 --> 01:19:46,445
We've talked enough.

1091
01:19:48,530 --> 01:19:50,199
- Mal.
- Come out onto the ledge...

1092
01:19:50,365 --> 01:19:53,076
- ...or I'll jump right now.
- Okay.

1093
01:19:57,331 --> 01:19:59,124
We're gonna talk about this.

1094
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:01,168
All right?

1095
01:20:01,335 --> 01:20:06,423
- I'm asking you to take a leap of faith.
- No, honey.

1096
01:20:07,382 --> 01:20:10,385
No, I can't. You know I can't do that.

1097
01:20:10,552 --> 01:20:13,305
Take a second, think about our children.

1098
01:20:13,472 --> 01:20:15,224
Think about James.

1099
01:20:15,974 --> 01:20:17,601
Think about Phillipa, now.

1100
01:20:17,768 --> 01:20:20,687
If I go without you,
they'll take them away anyway.

1101
01:20:20,854 --> 01:20:24,149
- What does that mean?
- I filed a letter with our attorney...

1102
01:20:24,608 --> 01:20:27,945
...explaining how I'm fearful
for my safety.

1103
01:20:30,155 --> 01:20:32,324
How you've threatened to kill me.

1104
01:20:35,118 --> 01:20:37,746
- Why did you do this?
- I love you, Dom.

1105
01:20:37,913 --> 01:20:39,665
Why did--? Why would you do this to me?

1106
01:20:39,831 --> 01:20:42,709
I've freed you from the guilt
of choosing to leave them.

1107
01:20:42,876 --> 01:20:45,921
We're going home to our real children.

1108
01:20:46,088 --> 01:20:49,716
Oh, no, no, no, Mal, you listen to me,
all right? Mal, look at me, please?

1109
01:20:49,883 --> 01:20:51,176
You're waiting for a train.

1110
01:20:51,677 --> 01:20:55,264
- Mal, goddamn it, don't do this!
- A train that will take you far away.

1111
01:20:55,430 --> 01:20:57,432
James and Phillipa are waiting for you!

1112
01:20:57,599 --> 01:21:01,436
You know where you hope this train
will take you, but you can't know for sure.

1113
01:21:01,603 --> 01:21:03,814
- Mal, look at me!
- But it doesn't matter.

1114
01:21:03,981 --> 01:21:07,192
- Mal, goddamn it! Mal, listen to me!
- Because you'll be together.

1115
01:21:07,359 --> 01:21:11,196
Sweetheart! Look at me! Mal, no!

1116
01:21:11,363 --> 01:21:12,781
Jesus Christ!

1117
01:21:16,285 --> 01:21:20,038
She had herself declared sane
by three different psychiatrists.

1118
01:21:20,205 --> 01:21:24,376
It made it impossible for me to try
to explain the nature of her madness.

1119
01:21:24,543 --> 01:21:25,794
<i>So I ran.</i>

1120
01:21:25,961 --> 01:21:27,462
[CHILDREN GIGGLING]

1121
01:21:27,629 --> 01:21:29,881
MAN:
Right now, or never, Cobb.

1122
01:21:36,221 --> 01:21:40,892
WOMAN:
James! Phillipa! Come on in! Come on!

1123
01:21:41,143 --> 01:21:42,227
All right, let's go.

1124
01:21:42,728 --> 01:21:46,565
I left my children behind and I've been
trying to buy my way back ever since.

1125
01:21:47,107 --> 01:21:50,986
Your guilt defines her.
It's what powers her.

1126
01:21:51,153 --> 01:21:54,448
But you are not responsible
for the idea that destroyed her.

1127
01:21:55,699 --> 01:21:58,243
And if we are gonna succeed in this...

1128
01:21:58,410 --> 01:22:03,415
...you have to forgive yourself,
and you're gonna have to confront her.

1129
01:22:03,582 --> 01:22:05,417
But you don't have to do that alone.

1130
01:22:05,584 --> 01:22:07,836
- No, you're not...
- I'm doing it for the others.

1131
01:22:08,503 --> 01:22:13,592
Because they have no idea the risk
they've taken coming down here with you.

1132
01:22:18,680 --> 01:22:20,182
We have to move.

1133
01:22:20,349 --> 01:22:21,767
Time's up.

1134
01:22:21,933 --> 01:22:24,686
All right. I don't know any combination.

1135
01:22:24,853 --> 01:22:27,939
- Not consciously, anyway.
COBB: How about instinctively, huh?

1136
01:22:28,106 --> 01:22:32,069
I got somebody in your father's office
right now ready to tap in the combination.

1137
01:22:32,235 --> 01:22:35,113
Give me the first six numbers
that come to your head right now.

1138
01:22:35,280 --> 01:22:37,532
- I have no idea.
- Right now!

1139
01:22:37,699 --> 01:22:39,951
I said, right now! Right now!

1140
01:22:40,118 --> 01:22:43,997
Five, two, eight, four, nine, one.

1141
01:22:47,876 --> 01:22:49,878
You'll have to do better than that.

1142
01:22:50,045 --> 01:22:53,382
All right. Bag them.
You're going for a ride.

1143
01:22:57,761 --> 01:23:01,890
FISCHER: We're worth more to you alive.
You hear me?

1144
01:23:07,145 --> 01:23:08,647
What'd you get?

1145
01:23:09,064 --> 01:23:11,608
Relationship with his father
is worse than we imagined.

1146
01:23:11,775 --> 01:23:12,818
This helps us how?

1147
01:23:12,984 --> 01:23:16,071
The stronger the issues,
the more powerful the catharsis.

1148
01:23:17,280 --> 01:23:20,367
- How are we gonna reconcile them?
- I'm working on that.

1149
01:23:20,534 --> 01:23:24,079
Work faster.
The projections are closing in quick.

1150
01:23:24,246 --> 01:23:27,833
We gotta break out of here
before we're totally boxed in.

1151
01:24:02,909 --> 01:24:03,952
Damn it.

1152
01:24:04,119 --> 01:24:07,122
You mustn't be afraid to dream
a little bigger, darling.

1153
01:24:23,889 --> 01:24:26,892
We need to shift his animosity
from his father to his godfather.

1154
01:24:27,058 --> 01:24:31,062
- Destroy his one positive relationship?
- No, repair his relationship with his father...

1155
01:24:31,229 --> 01:24:33,565
...whilst exposing his godfather's
true nature.

1156
01:24:33,732 --> 01:24:36,735
We should charge Fischer a lot more
than Saito for this job.

1157
01:24:36,902 --> 01:24:39,237
His security is gonna
get worse as we go deeper.

1158
01:24:39,404 --> 01:24:41,907
- I think we run with Mr. Charles.
- No.

1159
01:24:42,324 --> 01:24:43,742
EAMES: Who's Mr. Charles?
- Bad idea.

1160
01:24:43,909 --> 01:24:47,454
The second we get into that hotel
his security's gonna be all over us.

1161
01:24:47,621 --> 01:24:49,748
We run with Mr. Charles
like on the Stein job.

1162
01:24:49,915 --> 01:24:52,250
- You've done it before?
ARTHUR: Yeah, and it didn't work.

1163
01:24:52,417 --> 01:24:55,921
The subject realized he was dreaming
and his subconscious tore us to pieces.

1164
01:24:56,087 --> 01:24:58,089
Excellent. But you learned a lot, right?

1165
01:24:58,256 --> 01:25:00,383
- I need some kind of distraction.
EAMES: No problem.

1166
01:25:00,550 --> 01:25:02,761
How about a lovely lady
that I've used before?

1167
01:25:02,928 --> 01:25:04,971
Listen to me.
You drive carefully, all right?

1168
01:25:05,138 --> 01:25:07,599
Everything down there is
gonna be unstable as hell.

1169
01:25:12,145 --> 01:25:15,524
Don't jump too soon. We only got
one shot at that kick. We gotta make it.

1170
01:25:15,690 --> 01:25:18,985
I'll play the music to let you know
it's coming. The rest is on you.

1171
01:25:19,152 --> 01:25:20,946
- You ready?
- Ready!

1172
01:25:22,197 --> 01:25:23,281
Sweet dreams.

1173
01:25:23,448 --> 01:25:25,367
WOMAN:
Am I boring you?

1174
01:25:26,660 --> 01:25:29,871
I was telling you my story.
I guess it wasn't to your liking.

1175
01:25:31,706 --> 01:25:34,376
Um, I have a lot on my mind.

1176
01:25:35,919 --> 01:25:37,504
There goes Mr. Charles.

1177
01:25:42,217 --> 01:25:44,844
Mr. Fischer, right?

1178
01:25:45,011 --> 01:25:48,515
Pleasure to see you again.
Rod Green from marketing. I--

1179
01:25:48,682 --> 01:25:49,808
Hmm.

1180
01:25:49,975 --> 01:25:52,644
- And you must be?
- Leaving.

1181
01:25:55,897 --> 01:25:57,023
In case you get bored.

1182
01:26:02,279 --> 01:26:03,655
Must've blown you off.

1183
01:26:03,822 --> 01:26:06,533
That is, unless her phone number
really is only six digits.

1184
01:26:08,702 --> 01:26:13,164
Funny way to make friends,
someone stealing your wallet like that.

1185
01:26:15,834 --> 01:26:16,918
[SCOFFS]

1186
01:26:17,085 --> 01:26:19,421
Goddamn it. The wallet alone
is worth at least 500--

1187
01:26:19,588 --> 01:26:20,714
About $500, right?

1188
01:26:20,880 --> 01:26:23,842
Don't worry about it.
My people are already on it as we speak.

1189
01:26:24,009 --> 01:26:25,969
Who or what is Mr. Charles?

1190
01:26:26,177 --> 01:26:29,848
It's a gambit designed to turn Fischer
against his own subconscious.

1191
01:26:30,015 --> 01:26:31,349
ARIADNE:
And why don't you approve?

1192
01:26:31,516 --> 01:26:33,518
It involves telling the mark
that he's dreaming...

1193
01:26:33,685 --> 01:26:35,854
...which involves attracting
a lot of attention to us.

1194
01:26:36,021 --> 01:26:38,940
- Didn't Cobb say never to do that?
- Hmm.

1195
01:26:39,107 --> 01:26:40,859
So now you've noticed how much time...

1196
01:26:41,026 --> 01:26:43,695
...Cobb spends doing things
he says never to do.

1197
01:26:45,405 --> 01:26:47,198
Mr. Saito, can I have a moment?

1198
01:26:47,365 --> 01:26:49,784
- I'm sorry, but...
MAN: Hold on! Wait!

1199
01:26:51,119 --> 01:26:52,412
You look a bit perkier.

1200
01:26:52,579 --> 01:26:54,914
Very amusing, Mr. Eames.

1201
01:26:59,252 --> 01:27:00,879
[RUMBLING]

1202
01:27:01,171 --> 01:27:02,672
Turbulence on the plane?

1203
01:27:02,839 --> 01:27:04,549
No, it's much closer.

1204
01:27:04,716 --> 01:27:06,384
That's Yusuf's driving.

1205
01:27:07,260 --> 01:27:08,470
[TIRES SCREECHING]

1206
01:27:12,724 --> 01:27:14,059
[GLASSES CLINKING]

1207
01:27:14,726 --> 01:27:17,187
Um, I'm sorry.
Who did you say you were?

1208
01:27:17,354 --> 01:27:19,439
COBB:
Rod Green from marketing.

1209
01:27:19,606 --> 01:27:21,232
But that's not true at all, is it?

1210
01:27:23,318 --> 01:27:26,780
My name is Mr. Charles.
You remember me, don't you?

1211
01:27:26,988 --> 01:27:28,948
I'm the head of your security down here.

1212
01:27:30,241 --> 01:27:32,160
Get out on a different floor,
keep moving.

1213
01:27:32,327 --> 01:27:34,663
Dump the wallet.
Security will be looking for that.

1214
01:27:34,829 --> 01:27:38,333
- Okay.
- We need to buy Cobb a little more time.

1215
01:27:40,126 --> 01:27:41,336
[COUGHING]

1216
01:27:41,961 --> 01:27:43,546
Security, huh?

1217
01:27:45,965 --> 01:27:48,134
- You work for the hotel?
- No, no.

1218
01:27:48,301 --> 01:27:52,931
I specialize in a very specific type
of security.

1219
01:27:53,306 --> 01:27:54,974
Subconscious security.

1220
01:27:55,141 --> 01:27:57,268
You're talking about dreams?

1221
01:27:57,435 --> 01:28:00,271
Are you talking about, um, extraction?

1222
01:28:01,314 --> 01:28:02,732
I am here to protect you.

1223
01:28:02,899 --> 01:28:04,484
[GLASS SHATTERS]

1224
01:28:04,859 --> 01:28:06,444
[PEOPLE CHATTERING]

1225
01:28:13,284 --> 01:28:14,786
[CHATTERING STOPS]

1226
01:28:16,162 --> 01:28:18,707
Mr. Fischer, I'm here
to protect you in the event...

1227
01:28:18,873 --> 01:28:22,252
...that someone tries to access
your mind through your dreams.

1228
01:28:22,419 --> 01:28:25,046
You're not safe here.

1229
01:28:25,880 --> 01:28:27,465
They're coming for you.

1230
01:28:39,811 --> 01:28:41,229
[YELLS]

1231
01:28:45,817 --> 01:28:47,861
Strange weather, isn't it?

1232
01:28:52,699 --> 01:28:53,825
You feel that?

1233
01:28:55,827 --> 01:28:56,995
What's happening?

1234
01:28:57,162 --> 01:29:00,415
Cobb's drawing Fischer's attention
to the strangeness of the dream...

1235
01:29:00,582 --> 01:29:03,460
...which is making his subconscious
look for the dreamer.

1236
01:29:03,626 --> 01:29:06,671
For me. Quick, give me a kiss.

1237
01:29:11,676 --> 01:29:15,555
- They're still looking at us.
- Yeah, it was worth a shot.

1238
01:29:16,222 --> 01:29:18,266
We should probably get out of here.

1239
01:29:22,479 --> 01:29:23,563
[GUNFIRE]

1240
01:29:33,531 --> 01:29:37,118
COBB: You feel that? You've actually
been trained for this, Mr. Fischer.

1241
01:29:37,285 --> 01:29:41,289
Pay attention to the strangeness
of the weather, the shift in gravity.

1242
01:29:41,456 --> 01:29:44,626
None of this is real. You're in a dream.

1243
01:29:49,422 --> 01:29:51,674
Now, the easiest way
for you to test yourself...

1244
01:29:51,883 --> 01:29:55,929
...is to try and remember how you arrived
at this hotel. Can you do that?

1245
01:29:56,763 --> 01:29:58,056
Yeah, I...

1246
01:29:58,223 --> 01:30:00,725
COBB: No, breathe, breathe.
Remember your training.

1247
01:30:02,769 --> 01:30:06,105
Accept the fact that you're in a dream,
and I'm here to protect you. Go on.

1248
01:30:08,107 --> 01:30:09,484
Mm-hm.

1249
01:30:12,278 --> 01:30:14,113
- You're not real?
- No.

1250
01:30:14,280 --> 01:30:17,659
No. I'm a projection
of your subconscious.

1251
01:30:17,825 --> 01:30:19,911
I was sent here to protect you
in the event...

1252
01:30:20,078 --> 01:30:22,997
...that extractors
tried to pull you into a dream.

1253
01:30:23,164 --> 01:30:26,417
And I believe that's what's
going on right now, Mr. Fischer.

1254
01:30:27,502 --> 01:30:28,545
Yeah.

1255
01:30:28,711 --> 01:30:29,796
Okay.

1256
01:30:30,713 --> 01:30:31,923
Okay.

1257
01:30:34,759 --> 01:30:36,761
Can you get me out of here?

1258
01:30:37,679 --> 01:30:40,265
Right away. Follow me.

1259
01:30:54,487 --> 01:30:55,822
Hold on a second.

1260
01:31:00,785 --> 01:31:02,620
Jesus Christ! What are you doing?

1261
01:31:02,787 --> 01:31:05,039
These men were sent here
to abduct you, all right?

1262
01:31:05,206 --> 01:31:07,792
If you want my help,
you have to remain calm.

1263
01:31:09,502 --> 01:31:11,796
I need you to work with me, Mr. Fischer.

1264
01:31:23,516 --> 01:31:27,145
If this is a dream, I should just
kill myself to wake up, right?

1265
01:31:27,312 --> 01:31:29,397
I wouldn't do that if I were you,
Mr. Fischer.

1266
01:31:29,564 --> 01:31:33,067
I believe they have you sedated,
and if you pull that trigger...

1267
01:31:33,234 --> 01:31:34,402
...you may not wake up.

1268
01:31:35,528 --> 01:31:39,824
You may go into a further dream state.
Now, you know what I'm talking about.

1269
01:31:42,160 --> 01:31:43,828
You remember the training.

1270
01:31:44,996 --> 01:31:46,372
Remember what I said to you.

1271
01:31:47,248 --> 01:31:48,416
Give me the gun.

1272
01:32:00,219 --> 01:32:02,847
- This room should be directly below 528?
- Yeah.

1273
01:32:04,098 --> 01:32:05,516
[SAFE BEEPS]

1274
01:32:12,190 --> 01:32:13,691
Think, Mr. Fischer, think.

1275
01:32:13,858 --> 01:32:17,695
What do you remember
from before this dream?

1276
01:32:18,071 --> 01:32:20,698
There was, um, a lot of gunfire.

1277
01:32:20,865 --> 01:32:23,493
There was rain.

1278
01:32:24,035 --> 01:32:25,620
Uncle Peter.

1279
01:32:25,870 --> 01:32:29,540
- Oh, my God, we've been kidnapped.
- Where were they holding you?

1280
01:32:32,543 --> 01:32:35,838
- They had us in the back of a van.
- That explains the gravity shifts.

1281
01:32:36,047 --> 01:32:38,091
You're in the back of a van.
Keep going.

1282
01:32:38,257 --> 01:32:40,551
It had something to do with, um...

1283
01:32:41,177 --> 01:32:44,055
Something to do with a safe.

1284
01:32:44,681 --> 01:32:46,307
God, why is it so hard to remember?

1285
01:32:46,474 --> 01:32:49,185
It's like trying to remember a dream
after you've woken up.

1286
01:32:49,352 --> 01:32:51,062
Listen, it takes years of practice.

1287
01:32:51,229 --> 01:32:53,648
You and Browning have been pulled
into this dream...

1288
01:32:53,815 --> 01:32:56,567
...because they're trying to steal
something from your mind.

1289
01:32:56,734 --> 01:33:00,321
I need you to focus and try
and remember what that is.

1290
01:33:00,488 --> 01:33:02,115
What is it, Mr. Fischer? Think!

1291
01:33:02,281 --> 01:33:06,035
A combination. They demanded
the first numbers to pop into my head.

1292
01:33:06,202 --> 01:33:08,913
They're trying to extract a number
from your subconscious.

1293
01:33:09,080 --> 01:33:12,875
It can represent anything.
We're in a hotel right now.

1294
01:33:13,668 --> 01:33:16,796
We should try hotel rooms.
What was the number, Mr. Fischer?

1295
01:33:16,963 --> 01:33:19,799
Try and remember for me.
This is very important.

1296
01:33:19,966 --> 01:33:21,300
Five.

1297
01:33:22,260 --> 01:33:24,721
Five, two-- It was something,
it was a long number.

1298
01:33:24,887 --> 01:33:26,973
That's good. We can start there.
Fifth floor.

1299
01:33:27,140 --> 01:33:28,182
Yep.

1300
01:33:28,349 --> 01:33:32,395
- So do you use a timer?
- No, I have to judge it for myself.

1301
01:33:32,603 --> 01:33:35,857
While you're all asleep in 528,
I wait for Yusuf's kick.

1302
01:33:36,023 --> 01:33:38,151
- Well, how will you know?
- His music warns me.

1303
01:33:38,317 --> 01:33:42,280
And then when the van hits the barrier
of the bridge, that should be unmistakable.

1304
01:33:42,447 --> 01:33:45,616
So we get a nice synchronized kick.

1305
01:33:45,783 --> 01:33:48,202
If it's too soon,
we won't get pulled out.

1306
01:33:48,369 --> 01:33:51,622
But if it's too late,
I won't be able to drop us.

1307
01:33:51,789 --> 01:33:55,626
- Well, why not?
- Because the van will be in free fall.

1308
01:33:56,127 --> 01:33:58,963
- Can't drop you without gravity.
- Right.

1309
01:34:04,635 --> 01:34:06,846
They're with me. Go on.

1310
01:34:31,162 --> 01:34:32,205
Mr. Charles.

1311
01:34:32,371 --> 01:34:35,333
- Do you know what that is, Mr. Fischer?
- Yeah, I think so.

1312
01:34:35,500 --> 01:34:37,585
They were trying to put you under.

1313
01:34:38,002 --> 01:34:40,087
- I'm already under.
- Under again.

1314
01:34:40,254 --> 01:34:42,840
What do you mean,
a dream within a dream?

1315
01:34:43,424 --> 01:34:45,885
Hey. I see you've changed.

1316
01:34:46,052 --> 01:34:47,678
I'm sorry?

1317
01:34:48,179 --> 01:34:52,058
Oh, I'm sorry.
I mistook you for a friend.

1318
01:34:52,225 --> 01:34:53,518
Oh.

1319
01:34:53,684 --> 01:34:55,812
Good-looking fellow, I'm sure.

1320
01:34:56,479 --> 01:34:59,398
No, no, no. That's Fischer's
projection of Browning.

1321
01:34:59,565 --> 01:35:01,442
Let's follow him
and see how he behaves.

1322
01:35:01,609 --> 01:35:03,569
- Why?
- Because how he acts will tell us...

1323
01:35:03,736 --> 01:35:07,073
...if Fischer is starting to suspect
his motives the way we want him to.

1324
01:35:07,240 --> 01:35:08,282
Shh.

1325
01:35:08,449 --> 01:35:09,867
[DOOR OPENS]

1326
01:35:12,870 --> 01:35:15,373
- Uncle Peter.
- You said you were kidnapped together?

1327
01:35:15,540 --> 01:35:18,960
Well, not exactly. They already had him.
They were torturing him.

1328
01:35:19,126 --> 01:35:20,586
And you saw them torture him?

1329
01:35:28,135 --> 01:35:29,971
The kidnappers are working for you?

1330
01:35:30,930 --> 01:35:32,390
Robert.

1331
01:35:32,557 --> 01:35:36,394
You're trying to get that safe open?
To get the alternate will?

1332
01:35:36,811 --> 01:35:41,065
Fischer Morrow has been my entire life.
I can't let you destroy it.

1333
01:35:41,232 --> 01:35:43,568
I'm not gonna throw away
my inheritance.

1334
01:35:43,734 --> 01:35:47,905
I couldn't let you rise
to your father's last taunt.

1335
01:35:48,739 --> 01:35:51,742
- What taunt?
- The will, Robert. That will?

1336
01:35:51,909 --> 01:35:53,870
That's his last insult.

1337
01:35:54,036 --> 01:35:57,665
A challenge for you to build something
for yourself...

1338
01:35:57,832 --> 01:36:01,627
...by telling you you're not worthy
of his accomplishments.

1339
01:36:07,300 --> 01:36:12,680
- What, but that he was, um, disappointed?
- I'm sorry.

1340
01:36:14,056 --> 01:36:17,143
But he's wrong.

1341
01:36:17,894 --> 01:36:20,521
You can build a better company
than he ever did.

1342
01:36:20,688 --> 01:36:23,107
Mr. Fischer? He's lying.

1343
01:36:23,274 --> 01:36:25,401
- How do you know?
- Trust me, it's what I do.

1344
01:36:25,568 --> 01:36:28,321
He's hiding something,
and we need to find out what that is.

1345
01:36:29,530 --> 01:36:32,950
I need you to do the same thing to him
that he was going to do to you.

1346
01:36:35,494 --> 01:36:39,582
We'll enter his subconscious and find out
what he doesn't want you to know.

1347
01:36:40,833 --> 01:36:42,293
All right.

1348
01:36:42,710 --> 01:36:44,003
[MACHINE HISSING]

1349
01:36:48,299 --> 01:36:49,550
COBB: He's out.
- Wait.

1350
01:36:49,717 --> 01:36:51,802
Whose subconscious are
we going into exactly?

1351
01:36:51,969 --> 01:36:53,304
We're going into Fischer's.

1352
01:36:53,471 --> 01:36:56,140
I told him it was Browning's
so he'd be a part of our team.

1353
01:36:56,307 --> 01:36:58,643
He's gonna help us break
into his own subconscious.

1354
01:36:58,809 --> 01:37:00,394
COBB:
That's right.

1355
01:37:00,561 --> 01:37:02,647
Security's gonna run you down hard.

1356
01:37:02,813 --> 01:37:05,149
And I will lead them on a merry chase.

1357
01:37:05,691 --> 01:37:08,319
- Just be back before the kick.
- Go to sleep, Mr. Eames.

1358
01:37:13,532 --> 01:37:14,742
ARTHUR:
You good?

1359
01:37:17,912 --> 01:37:19,246
Hey. You ready?

1360
01:37:19,413 --> 01:37:23,000
Yes, yes. I'm fine. I'm ready.

1361
01:37:35,554 --> 01:37:36,889
ARIADNE:
Cobb?

1362
01:37:37,556 --> 01:37:40,017
Cobb? What's down there?

1363
01:37:43,354 --> 01:37:45,982
Hopefully, the truth
we want Fischer to learn.

1364
01:37:46,148 --> 01:37:48,025
I mean, what's down there for you?

1365
01:37:56,033 --> 01:37:57,410
[SHOTGUN COCKS]

1366
01:37:58,577 --> 01:38:00,079
[GUNFIRE THEN YUSUF YELLS]

1367
01:38:10,923 --> 01:38:12,383
[ELEVATOR DINGS]

1368
01:38:22,184 --> 01:38:23,436
[YUSUF YELLS]

1369
01:38:34,071 --> 01:38:36,157
[BOTH GRUNTING]

1370
01:38:59,221 --> 01:39:01,098
[METAL GROANING]

1371
01:39:01,265 --> 01:39:03,059
[SECURITY GUARD SCREAMING]

1372
01:39:11,776 --> 01:39:13,069
[YELLING]

1373
01:40:15,089 --> 01:40:16,090
[GUNSHOT]

1374
01:40:23,097 --> 01:40:24,473
[LAUGHS]

1375
01:40:24,640 --> 01:40:26,392
Did you see that?

1376
01:40:44,869 --> 01:40:46,704
COBB:
Eames, this is your dream.

1377
01:40:46,871 --> 01:40:49,999
I need you to draw the security away
from the complex, understand?

1378
01:40:50,166 --> 01:40:51,959
- Who guides Fischer in?
COBB: Not me.

1379
01:40:52,126 --> 01:40:54,587
If I know the route,
everything could be compromised.

1380
01:40:54,753 --> 01:40:57,298
- I designed the place.
COBB: No, you're with me.

1381
01:40:57,464 --> 01:40:59,008
SAITO:
I could do it.

1382
01:40:59,425 --> 01:41:01,886
All right. Brief him on the route
into the complex.

1383
01:41:02,052 --> 01:41:06,223
- Fischer, you'll be going with him.
- All right. What about you?

1384
01:41:06,390 --> 01:41:09,226
You keep this live.
I'll be listening in the whole time.

1385
01:41:09,393 --> 01:41:13,564
The windows on that upper floor are big
enough for me to cover you from that tower.

1386
01:41:13,731 --> 01:41:15,024
FISCHER:
You're not coming in?

1387
01:41:15,191 --> 01:41:17,526
In order to find out the truth
about your father...

1388
01:41:17,693 --> 01:41:20,738
...you're gonna need to break into
Browning's mind on your own.

1389
01:41:21,572 --> 01:41:23,073
SAITO:
Come on, Fischer!

1390
01:41:27,578 --> 01:41:29,580
[BUZZER SOUNDING]

1391
01:42:18,128 --> 01:42:21,799
- Sound the alarm! Sound the alarm!
- Go, go, go!

1392
01:42:21,966 --> 01:42:23,509
Move!

1393
01:42:25,052 --> 01:42:26,136
Go!

1394
01:42:31,308 --> 01:42:32,643
[GUNFIRE]

1395
01:42:41,318 --> 01:42:42,361
YUSUF:
Sod it.

1396
01:42:53,539 --> 01:42:55,207
I hope you're ready.

1397
01:42:55,499 --> 01:42:58,168
["NON, JE NE REGRETTE RIEN" PLAYING]

1398
01:43:01,797 --> 01:43:03,799
[SONG ECHOING]

1399
01:43:03,966 --> 01:43:05,718
No, it's too soon.

1400
01:43:08,512 --> 01:43:10,347
[SONG ECHOING]

1401
01:43:12,224 --> 01:43:13,434
Cobb, do you hear that?

1402
01:43:13,600 --> 01:43:17,187
I first noticed it about 20 minutes ago,
I thought it was the wind up here.

1403
01:43:17,688 --> 01:43:20,190
Yeah, I hear it. It's music.

1404
01:43:20,357 --> 01:43:21,775
So, what do we do?

1405
01:43:21,942 --> 01:43:23,360
We move fast.

1406
01:43:28,866 --> 01:43:31,076
Yusuf's 10 seconds from the jump.

1407
01:43:32,953 --> 01:43:35,331
Which gives Arthur three minutes.

1408
01:43:36,248 --> 01:43:38,375
- Hey!
- That's him!

1409
01:43:39,043 --> 01:43:40,753
- Which gives us what?
- Sixty minutes.

1410
01:43:40,919 --> 01:43:44,214
- Can they make that route in an hour?
- They have to climb to the terrace.

1411
01:43:44,381 --> 01:43:47,217
Then they need a new route,
a more direct route.

1412
01:43:50,220 --> 01:43:51,805
[SHOUTS INDISTINCTLY]

1413
01:43:58,437 --> 01:44:00,064
It's designed as a labyrinth.

1414
01:44:00,230 --> 01:44:02,733
There must be access routes
that cut through the maze.

1415
01:44:02,900 --> 01:44:04,485
Eames?

1416
01:44:08,405 --> 01:44:09,948
[GUNFIRE]

1417
01:44:27,591 --> 01:44:28,842
Did Eames add any features?

1418
01:44:29,009 --> 01:44:30,469
I don't think I should tell you.

1419
01:44:30,636 --> 01:44:32,930
We don't have time for this.
Did he add anything?

1420
01:44:33,388 --> 01:44:34,640
He added an air-duct system...

1421
01:44:34,807 --> 01:44:36,558
- ...that can cut through the maze.
- Good. Explain it to them.

1422
01:45:06,004 --> 01:45:07,422
[SECURITY GUARD GRUNTS]

1423
01:45:12,261 --> 01:45:13,804
Paradox.

1424
01:45:13,971 --> 01:45:15,472
[SCREAMS THEN THUDS]

1425
01:46:18,619 --> 01:46:20,120
[BOTH GASPING]

1426
01:46:23,707 --> 01:46:25,751
ARIADNE: What was that?
- The kick.

1427
01:46:26,376 --> 01:46:28,253
Cobb! Cobb, did we miss it?

1428
01:46:28,420 --> 01:46:30,130
Yeah, we missed it.

1429
01:46:30,297 --> 01:46:31,506
FISCHER:
Man.

1430
01:46:31,673 --> 01:46:35,969
Couldn't someone have dreamt up
a goddamn beach? Huh?

1431
01:46:36,136 --> 01:46:37,387
Uh-huh.

1432
01:46:38,138 --> 01:46:40,307
EAMES [OVER RADIO]:
<i>Well, what the hell do we do now?</i>

1433
01:46:40,474 --> 01:46:42,309
We finish the job before the next kick.

1434
01:46:42,476 --> 01:46:44,561
- What next kick?
- When the van hits the water.

1435
01:47:07,417 --> 01:47:10,879
WOMAN [ON RECORDING]: <i>if you'd like to
make a call, please hang up and try again.</i>

1436
01:47:11,046 --> 01:47:12,881
<i>If you need help, hang up...</i>

1437
01:47:13,090 --> 01:47:16,426
How do I drop you without gravity?

1438
01:47:18,929 --> 01:47:21,598
Arthur has a couple minutes,
and we have about 20.

1439
01:47:42,286 --> 01:47:43,704
[SAITO COUGHING]

1440
01:47:44,997 --> 01:47:46,665
You okay?

1441
01:47:51,128 --> 01:47:55,007
SOLDIER: Run! Turn around!
Move to base! Move to base!

1442
01:47:55,173 --> 01:47:56,717
Go, go, go!

1443
01:47:58,885 --> 01:48:00,053
There's something wrong.

1444
01:48:00,220 --> 01:48:02,556
They're heading your way,
like they know something.

1445
01:48:03,807 --> 01:48:06,018
Just buy us more time, all right?

1446
01:48:06,184 --> 01:48:08,020
- Come on!
- On my way.

1447
01:48:08,186 --> 01:48:09,896
SOLDIER:
Come on! Move!

1448
01:48:23,327 --> 01:48:24,953
[SILENCED GUNSHOT]

1449
01:48:36,840 --> 01:48:38,425
[BOTH GRUNTING]

1450
01:49:34,064 --> 01:49:35,607
All right.

1451
01:49:40,320 --> 01:49:41,863
[SILENCED GUNSHOT]

1452
01:49:47,786 --> 01:49:50,580
Okay. That's the antechamber
outside the strong room.

1453
01:49:50,747 --> 01:49:54,251
- Does the strong room have windows?
- It wouldn't be very strong if it did.

1454
01:49:54,418 --> 01:49:56,670
Let's hope Fischer likes
what he finds in there.

1455
01:49:58,296 --> 01:50:01,633
- Are those projections his subconscious?
- Yes.

1456
01:50:01,800 --> 01:50:05,178
- Are you destroying parts of his mind?
- No. They're just projections.

1457
01:50:18,900 --> 01:50:20,235
[COUGHS]

1458
01:50:24,364 --> 01:50:25,782
[SAITO COUGHING]

1459
01:50:31,455 --> 01:50:33,206
- We're here.
- You're clear, but hurry.

1460
01:50:33,373 --> 01:50:35,208
There's an entire army headed your way.

1461
01:51:02,027 --> 01:51:03,403
[WHISPERS]
I'm in.

1462
01:51:04,029 --> 01:51:05,238
[STATIC OVER RADIO]

1463
01:51:05,405 --> 01:51:06,865
[TURNS RADIO OFF]

1464
01:51:08,700 --> 01:51:09,826
SOLDIER:
There he is.

1465
01:51:52,410 --> 01:51:55,121
- There's someone in there.
- Fischer, it's a trap. Get out.

1466
01:51:56,289 --> 01:51:58,875
Come on. Come on, a little lower.

1467
01:52:04,589 --> 01:52:05,757
ARIADNE:
Cobb.

1468
01:52:06,383 --> 01:52:08,927
No, she is not real.

1469
01:52:09,553 --> 01:52:10,762
How do you know that?

1470
01:52:10,929 --> 01:52:14,558
She is just a projection.
Fischer. Fischer is real.

1471
01:52:17,394 --> 01:52:18,395
[GRUNTS]

1472
01:52:18,562 --> 01:52:20,105
Hello.

1473
01:52:24,776 --> 01:52:27,988
ARIADNE: Eames!
Eames, get to the antechamber now!

1474
01:53:11,906 --> 01:53:14,784
EAMES: What happened?
- Mal killed Fischer.

1475
01:53:17,037 --> 01:53:19,080
I couldn't shoot her.

1476
01:53:19,748 --> 01:53:23,126
There's no use in reviving him.
His mind's already trapped down there.

1477
01:53:23,293 --> 01:53:24,336
It's all over.

1478
01:53:26,087 --> 01:53:27,714
So that's it, then? We failed?

1479
01:53:28,340 --> 01:53:30,508
We're done. I'm sorry.

1480
01:53:34,387 --> 01:53:37,015
It's not me that doesn't get back
to my family, is it?

1481
01:53:37,724 --> 01:53:41,645
Shame. I wanted to know what was
gonna happen in there. I swear we had this.

1482
01:53:41,811 --> 01:53:43,855
Let's set the charges.

1483
01:53:45,690 --> 01:53:47,192
No, there's still another way.

1484
01:53:48,485 --> 01:53:51,905
- We have to follow Fischer down there.
- Not enough time.

1485
01:53:52,072 --> 01:53:54,908
No, but there will be enough time
down there.

1486
01:53:56,034 --> 01:53:57,160
And we will find him.

1487
01:53:57,327 --> 01:54:01,247
Okay, as soon as Arthur's music kicks in,
just use the defibrillator to revive him.

1488
01:54:01,414 --> 01:54:04,334
We can give him his own kick
down below.

1489
01:54:05,335 --> 01:54:07,045
Look, you get him in there.

1490
01:54:07,212 --> 01:54:10,090
As soon as the music ends,
you blow up the hospital...

1491
01:54:10,256 --> 01:54:13,051
...and we all ride the kick
back up the layers.

1492
01:54:14,260 --> 01:54:19,015
It's worth a shot, if Saito can hold
the guards <i>off</i> while I set the charges.

1493
01:54:19,182 --> 01:54:21,309
Saito will never make it, will he?

1494
01:54:23,353 --> 01:54:25,814
- Cobb, come on. We've gotta try this.
- Go for it.

1495
01:54:25,980 --> 01:54:28,900
If you are not back before the kick,
I am gone with or without you.

1496
01:54:29,067 --> 01:54:32,320
She's right. She's right.
Come on. Let's go.

1497
01:54:36,533 --> 01:54:38,743
Can I trust you to do
what needs to happen here?

1498
01:54:38,910 --> 01:54:40,120
Mai's gonna be down there.

1499
01:54:40,286 --> 01:54:42,706
I know where to find her.
She'll have Fischer.

1500
01:54:42,872 --> 01:54:46,793
- How do you know?
- Because she wants me to come after him.

1501
01:54:46,960 --> 01:54:49,129
She wants me back down there with her.

1502
01:55:15,905 --> 01:55:16,906
All right?

1503
01:55:24,164 --> 01:55:26,833
- This is your world?
- It was.

1504
01:55:27,625 --> 01:55:29,586
And this is where she'll be.

1505
01:55:30,295 --> 01:55:31,337
Come on.

1506
01:55:32,464 --> 01:55:33,465
01:55:32,384 --> 01:55:33,385

1507
01:56:18,343 --> 01:56:20,803
Saito. Saito.

1508
01:56:21,262 --> 01:56:25,225
I need you to take care of Fischer
while I go set some charges, okay?

1509
01:56:25,391 --> 01:56:28,811
No room for tourists on these jobs.

1510
01:56:31,481 --> 01:56:32,649
Don't be silly.

1511
01:57:05,139 --> 01:57:07,183
ARIADNE:
You built all this? This is incredible.

1512
01:57:07,684 --> 01:57:09,686
We built for years.

1513
01:57:10,728 --> 01:57:12,730
Then we started in on the memories.

1514
01:57:19,779 --> 01:57:21,197
This way.

1515
01:58:02,864 --> 01:58:04,949
COBB:
This was our neighborhood.

1516
01:58:05,116 --> 01:58:07,452
Places from our past.

1517
01:58:07,619 --> 01:58:09,412
That was our first apartment.

1518
01:58:09,579 --> 01:58:11,789
Then we moved to that building
right there.

1519
01:58:11,956 --> 01:58:14,500
After Mal became pregnant,
that became our home.

1520
01:58:14,667 --> 01:58:17,462
You reconstructed all of this
from memory?

1521
01:58:17,629 --> 01:58:19,922
Like I told you, we had lots of time.

1522
01:58:20,089 --> 01:58:21,674
ARIADNE:
What is that?

1523
01:58:23,217 --> 01:58:24,969
That's the house Mal grew up in.

1524
01:58:25,136 --> 01:58:27,597
- Well, will she be in there?
- No.

1525
01:58:27,930 --> 01:58:28,931
Come on.

1526
01:58:29,098 --> 01:58:32,435
We both wanted to live in a house,
but we loved this type of building.

1527
01:58:32,602 --> 01:58:35,563
In the real world,
we'd have to choose, but not here.

1528
01:58:40,943 --> 01:58:42,278
[GRUNTS]

1529
01:58:43,404 --> 01:58:44,614
[GUNFIRE]

1530
01:58:46,532 --> 01:58:47,617
[GRUNTS]

1531
01:58:59,045 --> 01:59:00,129
[BEEPS]

1532
01:59:09,347 --> 01:59:10,473
[BEEPS]

1533
01:59:14,936 --> 01:59:15,978
[BEEPS]

1534
01:59:16,145 --> 01:59:18,147
How are we gonna bring Fischer back?

1535
01:59:18,564 --> 01:59:20,983
We're gonna have to come up
with some kind of a kick.

1536
01:59:21,150 --> 01:59:22,568
What?

1537
01:59:23,569 --> 01:59:24,654
I'm gonna improvise.

1538
01:59:24,821 --> 01:59:29,492
Listen, there's something you should
know about me. About inception.

1539
01:59:30,827 --> 01:59:32,161
[BOTH GRUNTING]

1540
01:59:32,328 --> 01:59:33,538
[GUNFIRE]

1541
01:59:42,588 --> 01:59:46,467
An idea is like a virus. Resilient.

1542
01:59:47,301 --> 01:59:48,845
Highly contagious.

1543
01:59:49,011 --> 01:59:52,348
And the smallest seed
of an idea can grow.

1544
01:59:53,433 --> 01:59:55,393
It can grow to define...

1545
01:59:56,436 --> 01:59:58,020
...or destroy you.

1546
02:00:01,149 --> 02:00:03,901
MAL:
The smallest idea, such as:

1547
02:00:04,527 --> 02:00:06,863
"Your world is not real."

1548
02:00:07,155 --> 02:00:10,908
Simple little thought
that changes everything.

1549
02:00:12,160 --> 02:00:16,664
So certain of your world. Of what's real.

1550
02:00:16,831 --> 02:00:18,624
Do you think he is?

1551
02:00:20,418 --> 02:00:23,337
Or do you think he's as lost as I was?

1552
02:00:24,464 --> 02:00:25,965
COBB:
I know what's real, Mal.

1553
02:00:28,050 --> 02:00:30,344
No creeping doubts?

1554
02:00:31,053 --> 02:00:33,681
Not feeling persecuted, Dom?

1555
02:00:33,973 --> 02:00:35,308
Chased around the globe...

1556
02:00:35,475 --> 02:00:38,144
...by anonymous corporations
and police forces...

1557
02:00:38,311 --> 02:00:40,938
...the way the projections
persecute the dreamer?

1558
02:00:42,565 --> 02:00:44,066
Admit it.

1559
02:00:45,359 --> 02:00:48,196
You don't believe in one reality anymore.

1560
02:00:48,362 --> 02:00:49,906
So choose.

1561
02:00:50,072 --> 02:00:53,409
Choose to be here. Choose me.

1562
02:01:15,681 --> 02:01:16,724
[COUGHS]

1563
02:01:20,186 --> 02:01:21,437
You know what I have to do.

1564
02:01:21,604 --> 02:01:24,273
I have to get back to our children
because you left them.

1565
02:01:25,608 --> 02:01:27,151
Because you left us.

1566
02:01:27,318 --> 02:01:29,111
- You're wrong.
COBB: I'm not wrong.

1567
02:01:29,278 --> 02:01:31,322
You're confused.

1568
02:01:32,448 --> 02:01:34,492
Our children are here.

1569
02:01:35,284 --> 02:01:38,538
And you'd like to see their faces again,
wouldn't you?

1570
02:01:38,704 --> 02:01:41,791
Yes, but I'm gonna see them
up above, Mal.

1571
02:01:41,958 --> 02:01:43,251
[GUNFIRE]

1572
02:01:46,462 --> 02:01:47,505
[BEEPS]

1573
02:01:51,634 --> 02:01:53,135
[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

1574
02:01:53,302 --> 02:01:54,679
[SAITO COUGHING]

1575
02:01:55,429 --> 02:01:57,431
Up above?

1576
02:01:58,307 --> 02:02:01,978
Listen to yourself.
These are our children.

1577
02:02:02,478 --> 02:02:03,855
Watch.

1578
02:02:04,355 --> 02:02:06,065
James? Phillipa?

1579
02:02:06,232 --> 02:02:08,651
Don't do this, Mal. Please.
Those aren't my children.

1580
02:02:08,818 --> 02:02:11,320
You keep telling yourself that,
but you don't believe it.

1581
02:02:11,487 --> 02:02:13,364
- No, I know it.
- What if you're wrong?

1582
02:02:13,781 --> 02:02:16,075
What if I'm what's real?

1583
02:02:17,285 --> 02:02:19,495
You keep telling yourself what you know.

1584
02:02:21,080 --> 02:02:23,332
But what do you believe?

1585
02:02:24,292 --> 02:02:25,501
What do you feel?

1586
02:02:27,670 --> 02:02:29,088
Guilt.

1587
02:02:30,298 --> 02:02:32,508
I feel guilt, Mal.

1588
02:02:33,050 --> 02:02:37,013
And no matter what I do,
no matter how hopeless I am...

1589
02:02:37,179 --> 02:02:41,434
...no matter how confused,
that guilt is always there...

1590
02:02:41,601 --> 02:02:44,145
...reminding me of the truth.

1591
02:02:44,645 --> 02:02:45,938
What truth?

1592
02:02:48,357 --> 02:02:53,613
That the idea that caused you to question
your reality came from me.

1593
02:02:56,991 --> 02:03:00,119
You planted the idea in my mind?

1594
02:03:01,621 --> 02:03:03,122
What is she talking about?

1595
02:03:04,040 --> 02:03:09,295
The reason I knew inception was possible
was because I did it to her first.

1596
02:03:10,338 --> 02:03:13,299
- I did it to my own wife.
- Why?

1597
02:03:14,050 --> 02:03:15,551
We were lost in here.

1598
02:03:16,469 --> 02:03:21,098
<i>I knew we needed to escape,
but she wouldn't accept it.</i>

1599
02:03:24,310 --> 02:03:29,982
<i>She had locked something away,
something deep inside.</i>

1600
02:03:30,733 --> 02:03:34,904
<i>A truth that she had once known,
but chose to forget.</i>

1601
02:03:35,863 --> 02:03:37,990
And she couldn't break free.

1602
02:03:40,701 --> 02:03:43,412
<i>So I decided to search for it.</i>

1603
02:03:43,996 --> 02:03:48,876
<i>I went deep into the recess of her mind
and found that secret place.</i>

1604
02:03:49,043 --> 02:03:50,920
<i>And I broke in...</i>

1605
02:03:51,504 --> 02:03:53,673
<i>...and I planted an idea.</i>

1606
02:03:54,423 --> 02:03:58,177
<i>A simple little idea
that would change everything.</i>

1607
02:04:03,432 --> 02:04:05,768
<i>That her world wasn't real.</i>

1608
02:04:18,614 --> 02:04:21,784
That death was the only escape.

1609
02:04:29,208 --> 02:04:30,960
You're waiting for a train.

1610
02:04:31,127 --> 02:04:32,628
[METAL GROANING]

1611
02:04:33,295 --> 02:04:36,007
A train that'll take you far away.

1612
02:04:37,174 --> 02:04:40,177
You know where you hope
this train will take you...

1613
02:04:41,303 --> 02:04:43,305
...but you can't know for sure.

1614
02:04:44,473 --> 02:04:46,017
Yet it doesn't matter.

1615
02:04:47,476 --> 02:04:50,646
- Now, tell me why!
- Because you'll be together!

1616
02:04:54,984 --> 02:04:59,030
COBB: <i>But I never knew that that idea
would grow in her mind like a cancer...</i>

1617
02:04:59,196 --> 02:05:01,615
<i>...that even after she woke...</i>

1618
02:05:04,493 --> 02:05:06,996
That even after
you came back to reality...

1619
02:05:09,915 --> 02:05:12,877
...that you'd continue to believe
your world wasn't real.

1620
02:05:15,671 --> 02:05:18,049
That death was the only escape.

1621
02:05:18,215 --> 02:05:20,885
Mal, no! Jesus!

1622
02:05:21,052 --> 02:05:24,764
- You infected my mind.
- I was trying to save you.

1623
02:05:24,930 --> 02:05:26,348
You betrayed me.

1624
02:05:27,183 --> 02:05:30,853
But you can make amends.
You can still keep your promise.

1625
02:05:31,020 --> 02:05:34,065
We can still be together, right here...

1626
02:05:34,231 --> 02:05:36,692
...in the world we built together.

1627
02:05:43,032 --> 02:05:44,533
[COUGHING]

1628
02:05:52,541 --> 02:05:53,584
[BEEPS]

1629
02:05:53,751 --> 02:05:55,044
[GUNFIRE]

1630
02:06:12,561 --> 02:06:13,854
[GRUNTS]

1631
02:06:31,330 --> 02:06:33,916
["NON, JE NE REGRETTE RIEN" PLAYING]

1632
02:06:42,216 --> 02:06:43,801
[SONG ECHOING]

1633
02:06:49,640 --> 02:06:51,308
[DEFIBRILLATOR CHARGING]

1634
02:06:54,353 --> 02:06:55,604
[THUNDER CRASHING]

1635
02:06:57,523 --> 02:06:59,859
Cobb, we need to get Fischer.

1636
02:07:00,734 --> 02:07:02,027
You can't have him.

1637
02:07:03,070 --> 02:07:06,490
- If I stay here, will you let him go?
- What are you talking about?

1638
02:07:10,953 --> 02:07:12,788
Fischer is on the porch.

1639
02:07:13,372 --> 02:07:15,958
- Go check he's alive, Ariadne.
- Cobb, you can't do this.

1640
02:07:16,125 --> 02:07:18,794
Go check he's alive right now. Do it.

1641
02:07:32,308 --> 02:07:33,642
[ARIADNE GRUNTING]

1642
02:07:34,435 --> 02:07:37,521
He's here! And it's time,
but you have to come now!

1643
02:07:37,688 --> 02:07:41,358
COBB: You take Fischer with you, all right?
- You can't stay here to be with her.

1644
02:07:43,944 --> 02:07:46,655
I'm not. Saito's dead by now.

1645
02:07:46,822 --> 02:07:50,326
That means he's down here somewhere.
That means I have to find him.

1646
02:07:52,328 --> 02:07:55,414
I can't stay with her anymore,
because she doesn't exist.

1647
02:07:55,581 --> 02:07:59,001
I'm the only thing
you do believe in anymore.

1648
02:07:59,168 --> 02:08:00,502
No.

1649
02:08:01,670 --> 02:08:03,088
I wish.

1650
02:08:04,673 --> 02:08:07,176
I wish more than anything, but...

1651
02:08:08,302 --> 02:08:11,013
...I can't imagine you
with all your complexity...

1652
02:08:11,180 --> 02:08:13,724
...all your perfection,
all your imperfection.

1653
02:08:14,850 --> 02:08:15,851
- You all right?
- Yeah.

1654
02:08:16,018 --> 02:08:17,853
Look at you.

1655
02:08:19,104 --> 02:08:24,026
You're just a shade.
You're just a shade of my real wife.

1656
02:08:24,485 --> 02:08:27,112
And you were the best
that I could do, but...

1657
02:08:27,947 --> 02:08:30,366
...I'm sorry, you're just not good enough.

1658
02:08:30,532 --> 02:08:32,201
Does this feel real?

1659
02:08:32,618 --> 02:08:33,744
[GUNSHOT]

1660
02:08:34,578 --> 02:08:37,206
- What are you doing?
- Improvising.

1661
02:08:41,460 --> 02:08:42,711
[GASPING]

1662
02:08:45,798 --> 02:08:47,508
No, no, no!

1663
02:08:48,467 --> 02:08:50,886
In there, now. Go, go, go.

1664
02:09:35,723 --> 02:09:37,599
I was dis--

1665
02:09:39,310 --> 02:09:41,645
I was dis-- Disa--

1666
02:09:42,521 --> 02:09:45,065
- I know, Dad.
- Dis--

1667
02:09:51,071 --> 02:09:53,157
I know you were disappointed...

1668
02:09:53,949 --> 02:09:55,326
...I couldn't be you.

1669
02:09:55,743 --> 02:09:57,244
No.

1670
02:09:57,745 --> 02:09:58,954
No, no, no.

1671
02:09:59,997 --> 02:10:01,999
I was disappointed...

1672
02:10:03,751 --> 02:10:05,669
...that you tried.

1673
02:10:22,811 --> 02:10:24,104
[BEEPING]

1674
02:10:28,942 --> 02:10:30,611
What?

1675
02:10:40,037 --> 02:10:41,163
Come on, come on.

1676
02:11:11,402 --> 02:11:12,903
Dad?

1677
02:11:14,696 --> 02:11:15,739
[SOBBING]

1678
02:11:51,233 --> 02:11:54,319
That's the kick, Ariadne!
You have to go now!

1679
02:12:07,166 --> 02:12:10,586
Don't lose yourself!
Find Saito and bring him back!

1680
02:12:10,752 --> 02:12:12,421
I will!

1681
02:12:18,802 --> 02:12:19,803
02:12:18,722 --> 02:12:19,723

1682
02:12:55,506 --> 02:12:56,632
Yes.

1683
02:12:56,798 --> 02:12:59,843
You said you dreamt
that we'd grow old together.

1684
02:13:02,554 --> 02:13:04,056
But we did.

1685
02:13:05,974 --> 02:13:08,352
<i>We did. You don't remember?</i>

1686
02:13:13,023 --> 02:13:15,859
I miss you more than I can bear...

1687
02:13:18,237 --> 02:13:20,239
...but we had our time together.

1688
02:13:21,573 --> 02:13:23,659
And I have to let you go.

1689
02:13:25,744 --> 02:13:27,538
I have to let you go.

1690
02:14:18,797 --> 02:14:20,090
I'm sorry, Robert.

1691
02:14:41,987 --> 02:14:43,488
[ARTHUR YELLS]

1692
02:14:47,743 --> 02:14:51,413
You know, the will means that Dad
wanted me to be my own man...

1693
02:14:52,497 --> 02:14:54,541
...not just to live for him.

1694
02:14:56,126 --> 02:14:58,670
That's what I'm gonna do, Uncle Peter.

1695
02:15:08,430 --> 02:15:09,681
What happened?

1696
02:15:10,140 --> 02:15:11,850
- Cobb stayed.
- With Mal?

1697
02:15:12,017 --> 02:15:13,268
No, to find Saito.

1698
02:15:15,562 --> 02:15:17,314
He'll be lost.

1699
02:15:19,858 --> 02:15:21,693
No, he'll be all right.

1700
02:15:37,709 --> 02:15:39,670
SAITO:
Have you come to kill me?

1701
02:15:43,965 --> 02:15:46,426
I'm waiting for someone.

1702
02:15:50,889 --> 02:15:53,642
COBB:
Someone from a half-remembered dream.

1703
02:15:56,395 --> 02:15:57,813
Cobb?

1704
02:15:58,522 --> 02:15:59,564
[SCOFFS]

1705
02:15:59,731 --> 02:16:01,483
Impossible.

1706
02:16:02,025 --> 02:16:04,653
We were young men together.

1707
02:16:06,238 --> 02:16:08,573
I'm an old man.

1708
02:16:09,825 --> 02:16:11,868
Filled with regret.

1709
02:16:14,996 --> 02:16:17,708
Waiting to die alone.

1710
02:16:20,877 --> 02:16:23,046
I've come back for you.

1711
02:16:26,341 --> 02:16:30,178
To remind you of something.

1712
02:16:33,473 --> 02:16:35,851
Something you once knew.

1713
02:16:38,687 --> 02:16:40,939
That this world is not real.

1714
02:16:45,861 --> 02:16:50,365
To convince me to honor
our arrangement?

1715
02:16:51,199 --> 02:16:53,952
To take a leap of faith, yes.

1716
02:17:00,041 --> 02:17:01,376
Come back...

1717
02:17:03,920 --> 02:17:07,299
...so we can be
young men together again.

1718
02:17:11,052 --> 02:17:13,054
Come back with me.

1719
02:17:16,141 --> 02:17:17,559
Come back.

1720
02:17:27,319 --> 02:17:29,029
FLIGHT ATTENDANT:
Hot towel, sir?

1721
02:17:29,654 --> 02:17:32,532
We'll be landing in Los Angeles
in about 20 minutes.

1722
02:17:32,699 --> 02:17:34,993
Do you need immigration forms?

1723
02:17:37,078 --> 02:17:38,413
Thank you.

1724
02:17:38,914 --> 02:17:40,540
Hot towel, sir?

1725
02:17:41,541 --> 02:17:44,544
- No.
- Do you need immigration forms?

1726
02:18:55,907 --> 02:18:56,992
Welcome home, Mr. Cobb.

1727
02:18:58,118 --> 02:18:59,744
Thank you, sir.

1728
02:19:43,788 --> 02:19:45,165
Welcome.

1729
02:19:45,624 --> 02:19:47,042
This way.

1730
02:20:08,229 --> 02:20:11,691
MILES:
James? Phillipa?

1731
02:20:15,403 --> 02:20:16,696
Look who's here.

1732
02:20:22,285 --> 02:20:23,370
Hey.

1733
02:20:24,454 --> 02:20:27,165
- Hey, guys! Hey! How are you?
PHILLIPA: Daddy! Daddy!

1734
02:20:27,332 --> 02:20:29,584
JAMES: Daddy!
COBB: How are you?

1735
02:20:30,710 --> 02:20:32,295
Look what I've been building!

1736
02:20:32,462 --> 02:20:36,174
- What are you building?
JAMES: We're building a house on the cliff!

1737
02:20:36,341 --> 02:20:39,219
COBB: On the cliff? Come on, I want you
to show me. Can you show me?

1738
02:20:39,386 --> 02:20:41,930
JAMES: Let's go.
PHILLIPA: Come on, Daddy!

